window.DV_CONTENT = {"book": "Book One · Vinash", "author": "Dhruv", "released": 5, "next": {"n": 6, "date": ""}, "nextLead": "Chapter Six is on its way. Its date will be announced here.", "lede": "Seven tears open in the sky, one on each continent. Under the one above Dwarka, four ordinary people awaken: a man running his late father’s shop on borrowed money, an engineer who reads patterns nobody else trusts, an analyst who keeps everyone together, and an engineer who builds.", "premise": "Two months before the sky opens, seismometers around the world start recording earthquakes that are not happening. In Dwarka, a shopkeeper with a debt and an engineer with a fever feel the pulses in their bodies before anyone can explain them. Then seven tears open in the sky, one on each continent, and the things that come through wear the shapes of the oldest stories.", "chapters": [{"n": 1, "title": "Adya", "deva": "आद्य", "gloss": "the beginning", "pov": "Samyukt", "teaser": "A shop kept alive on borrowed money, and the first news of seismic signals with no earthquake behind them.", "summary": "A Monday in September. Samyukt runs his late father’s chemical trading shop with ₹38,412 in the account, a hotel invoice stuck on a portal, and a creditor who uses his father’s memory to collect a loan. Dinner with his mother Naina and sister Ishita, gaming with Raghav and Kabir, and the first news of seismic signals with no earthquake behind them. Something warm has settled under his ribs.", "words": 2770, "minutes": 12, "release": "2026-10-14", "html": "<p class=\"first\">At nine seventeen on Monday morning, Samyukt had thirty-eight thousand, four hundred and twelve <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"rupee\">rupees<\/span> in the business account and three people asking him for money.<\/p>\n<p>The first was his raw-material supplier.<\/p>\n<p>“No, Sharma <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"ji\">ji<\/span>, I understand,” Samyukt said, pressing the phone between his ear and shoulder while he searched through the papers covering his desk. “I’m not asking you to send the entire order on credit. Release half of it. I’ll transfer the balance as soon as the hotel payment comes.”<\/p>\n<p>Across the shop, sunlight fell through a dusty glass door and turned the suspended chemical mist pale gold. Blue drums stood against one wall, beside stacks of white cans waiting for labels. The sharp smell of disinfectant mixed with the artificial lemon fragrance from a leaking sample bottle. A ceiling fan pushed both smells around without improving either.<\/p>\n<p>“You said the same thing on Friday,” Sharma replied.<\/p>\n<p>“Because they said the same thing to me on Thursday.”<\/p>\n<p>“My company wants advance payment.”<\/p>\n<p>“And my customer wants thirty days after delivery, which becomes sixty days after delivery, which becomes ‘<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"sir-is-in-a-meeting\">Sir is in a meeting<\/span>.’ We are all trapped in the same beautiful system.”<\/p>\n<p>Sharma did not laugh. He usually did.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt found the supplier’s quotation under an unopened electricity bill. Sodium hypochlorite, surfactant, fragrance and empty five-litre cans. The total at the bottom was <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"indian-digit-grouping\">₹1,16,840<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>“Transfer eighty thousand today,” Sharma said. “Then I’ll see what I can do.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt looked at the banking app open on his computer.<\/p>\n<p>“I can do fifty.”<\/p>\n<p>“Seventy-five.”<\/p>\n<p>“Fifty-five, and you send the cans with the first load. I can’t supply liquid in my hands.”<\/p>\n<p>There was a pause.<\/p>\n<p>“Sixty-five.”<\/p>\n<p>“Sixty, Sharma <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"ji\">ji<\/span>. Right now. Before you change your mind and before I remember that another supplier exists.”<\/p>\n<p>This time Sharma gave a reluctant snort. “Sixty. But if the balance is not cleared this week, don’t call me with another story.”<\/p>\n<p>“It’s never a story. It’s a tragedy.”<\/p>\n<p>The call ended. Samyukt let the phone fall onto the desk and rubbed his face with both hands.<\/p>\n<p>He could transfer sixty thousand only by pushing the account into its overdraft and hoping the hotel released its payment before the next round of debits arrived. That was not business planning. That was throwing two stones into the air and hoping they struck each other instead of his head.<\/p>\n<p>His phone rang again.<\/p>\n<p>The screen showed <strong><span class=\"w\" data-g=\"maa\">Maa<\/span><\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>He answered at once. “<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"haan\">Haan<\/span>, <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"maa\">Maa<\/span>?”<\/p>\n<p>“You left without breakfast.”<\/p>\n<p>“I ate.”<\/p>\n<p>“Tea is not breakfast.”<\/p>\n<p>“There were two biscuits.”<\/p>\n<p>“Samyukt.”<\/p>\n<p>He smiled despite himself. “I’ll eat something.”<\/p>\n<p>“Properly.”<\/p>\n<p>“Properly.”<\/p>\n<p>“And call the landlord. He asked whether you had seen his message.”<\/p>\n<p>The second person asking for money had found him through his mother.<\/p>\n<p>The smile disappeared. “I saw it.”<\/p>\n<p>“Is everything all right?”<\/p>\n<p>“Everything is fine. Payment is coming today.”<\/p>\n<p>The lie came easily because it was almost the truth. Payment was supposed to come today. There was always a thin bridge between reassurance and dishonesty, and Samyukt had become very good at standing on it.<\/p>\n<p>“Come home on time,” Naina said. “Ishita wants to discuss something with you.”<\/p>\n<p>“What did she do?”<\/p>\n<p>“Why do you assume she did something?”<\/p>\n<p>“Experience.”<\/p>\n<p>His mother laughed softly. The sound loosened something in his chest.<\/p>\n<p>“Eight thirty,” she said.<\/p>\n<p>“I’ll try.”<\/p>\n<p>“That means nine thirty.”<\/p>\n<p>“Eight forty-five. Final offer.”<\/p>\n<p>“Eat.”<\/p>\n<p>She ended the call before he could negotiate further.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt opened his messaging app and stared at the hotel accountant’s last reply.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Will check and update.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>It had arrived four days ago.<\/p>\n<p>He typed a polite reminder, deleted it and called instead.<\/p>\n<p>The accountant answered after the fifth ring. “Yes?”<\/p>\n<p>“Good morning, sir. Samyukt from Samar Chemicals. I’m calling regarding invoice 2147.”<\/p>\n<p>Keys clicked at the other end. “That invoice is on hold.”<\/p>\n<p>“Why?”<\/p>\n<p>“Purchase-order mismatch.”<\/p>\n<p>“The quantity was revised after delivery. Your housekeeping manager signed the new <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"challan\">challan<\/span>.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then send the revised invoice.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt stopped moving.<\/p>\n<p>On his desk, beneath a catalogue he had meant to sort last week, lay a sheet with <strong>REVISED<\/strong> written across the top in his own handwriting.<\/p>\n<p>“I sent it,” he said, less confidently.<\/p>\n<p>“Not on the portal.”<\/p>\n<p>“I emailed it.”<\/p>\n<p>“Portal submission is mandatory.”<\/p>\n<p>He remembered opening the portal late one night, seeing the list of required fields and deciding he would do it the next morning when his head was clearer. Then a leaking drum had arrived. Then a customer had returned twenty cans because the labels were crooked. Then a creditor had called. Then it had become today.<\/p>\n<p>“If I upload it now, how soon can you release the payment?” he asked.<\/p>\n<p>“It will enter the next cycle.”<\/p>\n<p>“Which is?”<\/p>\n<p>“Friday.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt looked at the date on the monitor. Monday.<\/p>\n<p>“Sir, the material was delivered six weeks ago.”<\/p>\n<p>“And the correct invoice has not been submitted.”<\/p>\n<p>“The correction is one line.”<\/p>\n<p>“The system doesn’t know that.”<\/p>\n<p>“You know that.”<\/p>\n<p>Silence.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt felt heat rise into his face. The man’s indifference, the absurdity of payment for a completed delivery being held hostage by one form, his own stupid delay—everything pressed against the same weak point.<\/p>\n<p>“Sir, I have suppliers and deliveries waiting because your company has accepted the goods and—”<\/p>\n<p>He stopped. Anger would not move the payment. It would only give the accountant a reason to end the call.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt inhaled through his nose and let the breath settle low in his abdomen, the way he once had before releasing an arrow.<\/p>\n<p>“I apologise,” he said. “Tell me exactly what is missing. I’ll complete it while we’re on the phone.”<\/p>\n<p>The accountant’s voice softened by a degree. “Upload the revised invoice, signed <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"challan\">challan<\/span> and credit note. Mark the purchase-order revision in remarks.”<\/p>\n<p>For the next twelve minutes, Samyukt did precisely what he was told. The payment still would not arrive before Friday, but the accountant agreed to request an exception.<\/p>\n<p>It was not a victory. It was movement, and movement would have to do.<\/p>\n<p>By eleven, the supplier had received <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"rupee\">₹60,000<\/span>. The business account was overdrawn by <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"rupee\">₹21,588<\/span>. By noon, the landlord had sent another message. At twelve thirty, an old customer called to complain that the floor cleaner’s fragrance was weaker than the previous batch. At one fifteen, Samyukt ate cold <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"aloo-paratha\">aloo paratha<\/span> over the ledger while watching a delivery <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"tempo\">tempo<\/span> reverse within an inch of the front shutter.<\/p>\n<p>The third person asking for money arrived at three.<\/p>\n<p>Mr Sethi never raised his voice. Samyukt would have preferred shouting. Shouting was honest. Mr Sethi spoke with the patient disappointment of a schoolteacher discussing a capable child who had chosen to fail.<\/p>\n<p>He entered wearing a pale grey shirt despite the September heat. His gaze moved once around the shop: the half-filled drums, the packed cartons, the ageing computer, the framed photograph near the desk.<\/p>\n<p>Samar stood in that photograph with one hand on a newly painted signboard and the other around nineteen-year-old Samyukt’s shoulder. The board read <strong>SAMAR CHEMICALS — INDUSTRIAL &amp; HOUSEKEEPING SOLUTIONS<\/strong>. Both of them looked exhausted and absurdly proud.<\/p>\n<p>Mr Sethi sat without being invited.<\/p>\n<p>“How is Naina <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"ji\">ji<\/span>?” he asked.<\/p>\n<p>“She’s well.”<\/p>\n<p>“And your sister?”<\/p>\n<p>“Also well.”<\/p>\n<p>“Good. Family should be well.” He folded his hands over his stomach. “Your father understood that.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt’s jaw tightened.<\/p>\n<p>The original ₹5 <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"lakh\">lakh<\/span> loan had Samar’s signature on it. Years of interest, partial payments and missed months had turned it into something that behaved less like a number and more like weather. Always present. Occasionally violent.<\/p>\n<p>“I transferred twenty-five thousand last month,” Samyukt said.<\/p>\n<p>“Interest was due before that.”<\/p>\n<p>“I know what was due.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then you know why I’m here.”<\/p>\n<p>“A large payment is clearing this week.”<\/p>\n<p>“You said that last week.”<\/p>\n<p>“Because it was supposed to clear last week.”<\/p>\n<p>Mr Sethi glanced again at Samar’s photograph. “Your father also trusted people’s promises.”<\/p>\n<p>The heat in Samyukt’s face returned, faster this time.<\/p>\n<p>“If you came to discuss repayment, discuss repayment. Don’t talk about my father.”<\/p>\n<p>Mr Sethi studied him. Then he smiled—not warmly, but with recognition. He had found the boundary.<\/p>\n<p>“Forty thousand by Saturday,” he said. “After that, we’ll discuss a new schedule.”<\/p>\n<p>“Twenty-five.”<\/p>\n<p>“Forty.”<\/p>\n<p>“Thirty, and I’ll give you the next date in writing.”<\/p>\n<p>“Your writing has not been especially profitable for either of us.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt’s fingers curled beneath the desk.<\/p>\n<p>He wanted to stand. He wanted to tell the man exactly what kind of person used someone’s dead father to frighten him. Instead, he looked at the cartons waiting to be dispatched and thought of the rent, Ishita’s plans and his mother counting grocery money without letting him see.<\/p>\n<p>“Thirty-five,” he said.<\/p>\n<p>Mr Sethi rose. “Saturday.”<\/p>\n<p>At the door, he stopped beside the photograph.<\/p>\n<p>“Samar was a good man,” he said. “Good men often leave complicated accounts.”<\/p>\n<p>When he was gone, Samyukt remained behind the desk for several seconds.<\/p>\n<p>Then he opened the bottom drawer, took out a roll of billing paper and threw it hard against the wall.<\/p>\n<p>It bounced once and landed under a chair.<\/p>\n<p>“Excellent,” he muttered. “Very frightening.”<\/p>\n<p>He retrieved it, because billing paper cost money.<\/p>\n<p>At eight twenty-six that evening, Samyukt pulled down the shop shutter. His shoulders ached as he fitted the lock. The market around him was still bright—pharmacies, <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"coaching-centres\">coaching centres<\/span>, food stalls and <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"mobile-shops\">mobile shops<\/span> fighting the darkness with white LEDs and handwritten offers. Scooters squeezed through gaps that did not exist. Someone nearby was frying <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"momos\">momos<\/span>. A delivery rider argued with a security guard while three people gave both of them directions.<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"w\" data-g=\"dwarka\">Dwarka<\/span> moved around him with the confidence of a place expecting tomorrow to resemble today.<\/p>\n<p>His phone vibrated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Raghav:<\/strong> Game tonight. No excuses.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Kabir:<\/strong> He will say he has work.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Raghav:<\/strong> He always has work. We are rescuing him from capitalism.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt typed while walking towards his motorcycle.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Samyukt:<\/strong> Capitalism has better aim than you.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Raghav:<\/strong> Personal attack. 10 pm.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Kabir:<\/strong> 10:30. Raghav saying 10 means 10:30.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Raghav:<\/strong> Betrayal from inside the house.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt smiled and put the phone away.<\/p>\n<p>Home was a two-bedroom rented flat less than fifteen minutes from the shop, although the journey took longer on evenings when every red light seemed to choose him personally. When he opened the door at eight fifty-three, Ishita was sitting cross-legged on the floor with her laptop, three notebooks and an expression usually associated with court appeals.<\/p>\n<p>She looked at the wall clock. “Eight forty-five, final offer.”<\/p>\n<p>“Traffic.”<\/p>\n<p>“The national excuse.”<\/p>\n<p>Naina emerged from the kitchen carrying a steel bowl. “Wash your hands. Food is ready.”<\/p>\n<p>“I need to change.”<\/p>\n<p>“Hands first.”<\/p>\n<p>“I’m twenty-five.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then by now you should know to wash them without being told.”<\/p>\n<p>He obeyed.<\/p>\n<p>Dinner was <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"rajma\">rajma<\/span>, rice and the onion salad that Naina always made too spicy and insisted was perfectly fine. Samyukt ate his first serving quickly enough that she put more rice on his plate without asking.<\/p>\n<p>“Slowly,” she said. “No one is taking it away from you.”<\/p>\n<p>“I might,” Ishita said.<\/p>\n<p>“You already took half the <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"rajma\">rajma<\/span>.”<\/p>\n<p>“Psychology graduates require protein.”<\/p>\n<p>“You graduated with a BA, not from a wrestling academy.”<\/p>\n<p>Ishita nudged her laptop towards him. An application form filled the screen.<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"ma-entrances\">MA entrances<\/span>,” she said. “Two forms close this week.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then fill both.”<\/p>\n<p>“Both have fees.”<\/p>\n<p>“How much?”<\/p>\n<p>She told him. It was not a large amount. Today, every amount had become large.<\/p>\n<p>“Fill them,” he repeated.<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"bhai\">Bhai<\/span>, I can wait. I’m also applying for jobs.”<\/p>\n<p>“Do both.”<\/p>\n<p>“I don’t want you paying for everything.”<\/p>\n<p>“Good. When you become a famous psychologist, I’ll send all my bills to you.”<\/p>\n<p>“You need a psychologist before that.”<\/p>\n<p>“I live with one.”<\/p>\n<p>“Exactly. Free treatment, still no improvement.”<\/p>\n<p>Naina smiled, but her eyes stayed on Samyukt. “Did the payment come?”<\/p>\n<p>He took a drink of water.<\/p>\n<p>“It’s processed. Friday, most likely.”<\/p>\n<p>Not a lie. Not completely.<\/p>\n<p>“And the landlord?” she asked.<\/p>\n<p>“Handled.”<\/p>\n<p>“Samyukt—”<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"maa\">Maa<\/span>.” He tried to keep his tone gentle. “I said I’ll manage it.”<\/p>\n<p>The words came out more sharply than he intended. Naina lowered her eyes to her plate.<\/p>\n<p>The silence lasted only a moment, but he hated himself inside it.<\/p>\n<p>“Sorry,” he said. “Long day.”<\/p>\n<p>She placed another spoonful of <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"rajma\">rajma<\/span> on his plate. “That is why you should be home on Sundays.”<\/p>\n<p>He almost told her about Mr Sethi. He could have said the number aloud—thirty-five thousand by Saturday—and watched the burden become something shared.<\/p>\n<p>Instead, he said, “This Sunday. Promise.”<\/p>\n<p>After dinner, Ishita returned to her applications. Naina watched a <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"television-serial\">television serial<\/span> at low volume. Samyukt sat at the small table near the balcony and opened the old ledger he had brought home from the shop.<\/p>\n<p>Most of its pages were in his father’s handwriting: rounded Hindi notes, hurried English abbreviations and customers’ names followed by small comments that no accounting software would accept.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Paid late, but daughter’s wedding. Give time.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Good man. Business weak. Do not pressure.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Brother’s friend. Help if possible.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Samar had built the business from nothing and then treated its money as if every relative’s emergency had a legal claim over it. Samyukt had spent six years resenting that kindness and trying to preserve it at the same time.<\/p>\n<p>On the inside cover, his father had written a sentence in blue ink:<\/p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"w\" data-g=\"neki-kar-dariya-mein-daal\">Neki kar, dariya mein daal<\/span>.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Do good and cast it into the river.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt ran his thumb over the words.<\/p>\n<p>His father’s last meaningful advice returned to him often. It had come during an ordinary conversation, long before breathlessness, traffic lights and a desperate drive to the hospital turned every earlier moment into a final one.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Never leave kindness, empathy and compassion behind. You will not find happiness without them.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>He shut the ledger.<\/p>\n<p>At ten thirty-seven, he put on his headset and joined the game.<\/p>\n<p>“The industrialist arrives,” Raghav announced. “How many multinational corporations did you acquire today?”<\/p>\n<p>“Three,” Samyukt said. “I rejected a fourth. Their work culture was poor.”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir’s voice came through more quietly. “You sound tired.”<\/p>\n<p>“He always sounds tired,” Raghav said. “That’s his brand.”<\/p>\n<p>“I’m fine.”<\/p>\n<p>“Payment came?” Kabir asked.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt selected his character and stared at the loading screen. Kabir remembered things people hoped he had forgotten.<\/p>\n<p>“Friday.”<\/p>\n<p>Neither friend spoke for a second.<\/p>\n<p>Then Raghav said, “All right. Tonight we lose because of skill, not depression.”<\/p>\n<p>“We lose because you rush alone,” Kabir replied.<\/p>\n<p>“I create opportunities.”<\/p>\n<p>“For the enemy.”<\/p>\n<p>The first match began. For forty minutes, Samyukt’s world became clean and immediate: footsteps through the headset, ammunition counts, doorways, angles, the position of two people he trusted. Raghav pushed every fight as though hesitation were a moral failure. Kabir repaired bad decisions while complaining about them. Samyukt watched the edges, noticed movement first and still followed Raghav into situations all three of them knew were foolish.<\/p>\n<p>They lost twice and won once.<\/p>\n<p>During the next loading screen, Ishita raised the television volume in the other room.<\/p>\n<p>“…recorded across multiple seismic stations,” a news anchor was saying. “However, officials have clarified that no earthquake occurred.”<\/p>\n<p>“One minute,” Samyukt said, pulling one side of the headset away.<\/p>\n<p>On television, a scientist stood beside a graph of thin, repeating spikes.<\/p>\n<p>“The signal resembles a low-frequency seismic event,” she explained, “but we have found no corresponding epicentre, surface movement or known tectonic cause. At present, there is no reason for public alarm.”<\/p>\n<p>Ishita looked back at him. “Earthquake without an earthquake?”<\/p>\n<p>“Instrument error,” Samyukt said.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir had heard the broadcast through Samyukt’s microphone. “At multiple stations?”<\/p>\n<p>“Congratulations,” Raghav said. “The earth has discovered bugs.”<\/p>\n<p>The anchor moved on to a political debate. Ishita lowered the volume again.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt put his headset back on, but the next match did not hold him. The room felt close despite the open balcony door. He got up and stepped outside.<\/p>\n<p>Below, <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"dwarka\">Dwarka<\/span> was settling into its usual night sounds—distant horns, a dog barking and the whistle of a <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"pressure-cooker\">pressure cooker<\/span> in another flat. Nothing shook. Through the open door, the water in the glass on his table remained perfectly still.<\/p>\n<p>Yet the air felt heavier than it had that morning.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt drew a slow breath into his abdomen.<\/p>\n<p>It did not settle him.<\/p>\n<p>For no reason he could name, he looked up.<\/p>\n<p>The sky above the apartment blocks was clear and empty.<\/p>\n<p>His phone vibrated in his pocket.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Kabir:<\/strong> It happened again. Same pattern. Same interval.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt read the message twice.<\/p>\n<p>Before he could reply, a dull heat gathered beneath his ribs. It was gone within a breath, but for that brief moment it had felt almost deliberate.<\/p>\n<p>He pressed a hand against the spot and waited.<\/p>\n<p>Nothing happened.<\/p>\n<p>Below him, <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"dwarka\">Dwarka<\/span> continued as though nothing had changed.<\/p>"}, {"n": 2, "title": "Aakanksha", "deva": "आकांक्षा", "gloss": "aspiration", "pov": "Kairavi", "teaser": "Kairavi lines up four seismic stations overnight and finds the pulses are speeding up. By morning she has a fever.", "summary": "Kairavi, an AI engineer, refuses to trust a model that looks too good and finds the filter that was flattering it. At home she gives her father Rajeev ten thousand of the fifteen he asked for, and they reopen the oldest argument in the house: the astrophysics degree abroad that her family said no to. Overnight she lines up four seismic stations and finds that the pulses are speeding up. By morning she has a fever.", "words": 3329, "minutes": 14, "release": "2026-10-14", "html": "<p class=\"first\">Kairavi knew the model was lying because it failed too neatly.<\/p>\n<p>The accuracy chart on her monitor showed ninety-four point eight per cent. The line was smooth, the test results were stable and the presentation waiting for the afternoon review already contained the phrase <strong>production-ready<\/strong> in three different places.<\/p>\n<p>Everyone in the meeting room wanted the number to be true.<\/p>\n<p>That was the first reason she distrusted it.<\/p>\n<p>“Run the failed cases again,” she said.<\/p>\n<p>Mitali stopped halfway through closing her laptop. “All of them?”<\/p>\n<p>“Only the false negatives.”<\/p>\n<p>“There are twelve thousand.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then the first five hundred.”<\/p>\n<p>At the far end of the table, Varun looked up from his phone. “The evaluation pipeline has already sampled them.”<\/p>\n<p>“Randomly.”<\/p>\n<p>“That is generally how sampling works.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi turned her screen towards him. “And it says the model performs equally well across all user groups. That is generally not how anything works.”<\/p>\n<p>Mitali covered a smile with her coffee mug.<\/p>\n<p>It was Tuesday morning, and the office air-conditioning had decided that <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"dwarka\">Dwarka<\/span> was located somewhere near the Arctic Circle. Kairavi pulled the sleeves of her rust-coloured blazer over her wrists. Through the glass wall behind Varun, the rest of the floor moved through its usual rhythm—keyboards, muted calls, rolling chairs and people carrying coffee as if it were emergency medicine.<\/p>\n<p>Their model sorted customer-support messages by urgency. It was meant to recognise complaints that required immediate human attention: failed payments, locked accounts, suspected fraud. In three weeks, if the review went well, it would begin handling messages from millions of users.<\/p>\n<p>Ninety-four point eight per cent sounded excellent in a presentation.<\/p>\n<p>The remaining five point two per cent sounded different when one of those errors could represent someone’s missing salary.<\/p>\n<p>Mitali reopened her laptop. “Five hundred coming up.”<\/p>\n<p>Varun sighed. “The review is at four.”<\/p>\n<p>“Good,” Kairavi said. “We have six hours to discover why I’m wrong.”<\/p>\n<p>“And if you’re not?”<\/p>\n<p>“Then we have six hours to remove ‘production-ready’ from the deck.”<\/p>\n<p>He gave her the look people reserved for colleagues who had created work before lunch.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi returned to her desk.<\/p>\n<p>Her first guess was that the training data was old. It was a familiar problem: users changed the way they spoke faster than companies changed the way they labelled speech. She compared the newest messages with the training set and found differences, but nothing large enough to explain the pattern.<\/p>\n<p>Her second guess was language detection. That failed too. Hindi messages were not performing worse than English ones.<\/p>\n<p>By eleven thirty, she had disproved both of her own theories and developed a headache behind her right eye.<\/p>\n<p>“Breakfast?” Mitali asked, appearing beside her with two paper plates of <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"poha\">poha<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>“Had it.”<\/p>\n<p>Mitali looked at the unopened granola bar near Kairavi’s keyboard.<\/p>\n<p>“That is evidence,” Kairavi said.<\/p>\n<p>“That is packaging.”<\/p>\n<p>Mitali placed one plate beside the keyboard and sat on the edge of the next desk. She worked in data engineering, wore silver eyeliner before noon and treated every office crisis as a story she would later improve for entertainment.<\/p>\n<p>“You were online at two thirteen last night,” she said.<\/p>\n<p>“How do you know?”<\/p>\n<p>“You replied to my message.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then why were you online?”<\/p>\n<p>“I was making poor decisions. You were correcting my grammar.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi pushed the plate away from the laptop and took a bite. “There was that seismic report.”<\/p>\n<p>“The earthquake that wasn’t an earthquake?”<\/p>\n<p>“It was detected in more than one location. Instrument error doesn’t usually coordinate across stations.”<\/p>\n<p>“Maybe the instruments have formed a union.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then management should be worried.”<\/p>\n<p>Mitali leaned closer. “Was it aliens?”<\/p>\n<p>“Yes. They crossed the universe to make a graph and leave.”<\/p>\n<p>“Scientists. No social skills.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi smiled, but the pain behind her eye pulsed again. She had slept badly. Not because of the report—at least, that was what she had told herself—but because the room had felt strangely close all night. Twice she had opened the window. Twice she had checked the air-conditioner. Nothing had changed the pressure sitting on her chest.<\/p>\n<p>She had woken before dawn with a mild fever and an ache running from the base of her neck to her wrists. By morning, the fever was gone. The ache remained.<\/p>\n<p>Too much screen time, she had decided.<\/p>\n<p>Too little sleep.<\/p>\n<p>Both explanations were convenient enough to accept.<\/p>\n<p>“Found your five hundred,” Mitali said, returning to her own screen. “Shared folder.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi opened the file.<\/p>\n<p>She read the first twenty failures, then the next twenty. Fraud complaints incorrectly marked as routine. Payment failures treated as general questions. Urgent messages assigned low confidence because they contained spellings the model had not seen often enough.<\/p>\n<p>There was no obvious connection.<\/p>\n<p>She filtered by language. Nothing.<\/p>\n<p>By account type. Nothing.<\/p>\n<p>By message length.<\/p>\n<p>The rows rearranged themselves.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi stopped chewing.<\/p>\n<p>The shorter messages were performing worse, but only after a particular preprocessing step. She opened the original text beside the version being sent into the model.<\/p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"w\" data-g=\"mera-paisa-kat-gaya\">Mera paisa kat gaya but payment nahi hua<\/span>.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>The processed version had removed half the sentence.<\/p>\n<p>She checked another.<\/p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"w\" data-g=\"card-block-karo\">Card block karo someone is using it<\/span>.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Again, the English words remained while the <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"romanised-hindi\">Romanised Hindi<\/span> had been stripped away as noise. The system had not failed to understand the users. It had never been allowed to hear them.<\/p>\n<p>“Mitali.”<\/p>\n<p>There was something in Kairavi’s voice that made her stand immediately.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi brought up the preprocessing rules. Months ago, a language filter had been added to clean corrupted text. A later update had expanded it. Individually, both changes made sense. Together, they erased unrecognised words before classification.<\/p>\n<p>“It’s code-switching,” Kairavi said. “The evaluation set is balanced by language, but real users don’t speak in one language at a time.”<\/p>\n<p>Mitali scanned the examples. “And our cleaned test set already passed through this filter.”<\/p>\n<p>“So we tested the model on the evidence we hadn’t deleted.”<\/p>\n<p>Varun had joined them without either of them noticing. “Can we patch it before four?”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi looked at the clock.<\/p>\n<p>“We can disable the rule and rerun validation. A proper fix will take longer.”<\/p>\n<p>“How much does the accuracy drop?”<\/p>\n<p>“I don’t know yet.”<\/p>\n<p>“Estimate.”<\/p>\n<p>“Enough that the review needs the truth.”<\/p>\n<p>He pressed his tongue against the inside of his cheek. The presentation, the timeline and someone’s quarterly target were rearranging themselves behind his eyes.<\/p>\n<p>“Do it,” he said at last.<\/p>\n<p>The new accuracy was eighty-seven point one per cent.<\/p>\n<p>At four, Varun removed <strong>production-ready<\/strong> from the presentation.<\/p>\n<p>At four forty, the review panel postponed deployment.<\/p>\n<p>At five ten, Kairavi was congratulated for preventing a bad release by the same people who had spent most of the day hoping she would fail to find anything.<\/p>\n<p>At six twenty, she and Mitali left the office in search of fried food.<\/p>\n<p>They found it at a crowded stall beneath the <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"metro\">metro<\/span> line, where oil hissed in a blackened pan and the vendor packed <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"chilli-potatoes\">chilli potatoes<\/span> into paper bowls faster than the customers could order them. Evening traffic moved beyond them in red lines. The concrete pillars held old posters, fresh dust and the deep vibration of trains arriving overhead.<\/p>\n<p>Mitali lifted her fork. “To reduced accuracy.”<\/p>\n<p>“To delayed promotions.”<\/p>\n<p>“To being right in rooms where it is inconvenient.”<\/p>\n<p>They tapped wooden forks together.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi had taken only two bites when her phone rang.<\/p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"w\" data-g=\"papa\">Papa<\/span><\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>She watched it ring once before answering.<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"haan\">Haan<\/span>?”<\/p>\n<p>“Where are you?” Rajeev asked.<\/p>\n<p>No hello. No question about her day. Location first.<\/p>\n<p>“Outside the office. I’m eating with Mitali.”<\/p>\n<p>“Your mother made dinner.”<\/p>\n<p>“I’ll eat a little at home.”<\/p>\n<p>“Come soon. Aarohi needs help with some course forms.”<\/p>\n<p>“She could have called me.”<\/p>\n<p>“I am calling you.”<\/p>\n<p>Behind his voice came the electronic beep of a <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"billing-counter\">billing counter<\/span> and someone asking for a <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"carry-bag\">carry bag<\/span>. He was still at the supermarket.<\/p>\n<p>“I’ll be home in forty minutes,” she said.<\/p>\n<p>“And transfer fifteen thousand to the household account. The refrigerator payment is due.”<\/p>\n<p>“I transferred twenty-five at the start of the month.”<\/p>\n<p>“Expenses don’t ask whether the month has started or ended.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi looked at Mitali, who politely became very interested in the traffic.<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"papa\">Papa<\/span>, I have already contributed what we agreed. If the house needs more, we can discuss it when I get home.”<\/p>\n<p>“What is there to discuss?”<\/p>\n<p>“Whether I can afford the extra amount and whether I agree to it.”<\/p>\n<p>The <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"billing-counter\">billing counter<\/span> beeped again.<\/p>\n<p>“Come home,” Rajeev said, and ended the call.<\/p>\n<p>Mitali waited.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi stabbed a piece of potato hard enough to split the paper bowl. “Not a word.”<\/p>\n<p>“I was going to say you have excellent taste in street food.”<\/p>\n<p>“Liar.”<\/p>\n<p>“Ninety-four point eight per cent truthful.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi laughed despite herself. She stayed for another ten minutes, finished the food and hated that doing so felt like rebellion.<\/p>\n<p>Home was on the fourth floor of an apartment building whose lift displayed every floor except the one it had actually reached. When Kairavi entered, music was playing from Aarohi’s room, the television was arguing in the living room and Meera was arguing back from the kitchen.<\/p>\n<p>“If they invite six people to debate,” her mother called, “they should allow at least one to finish a sentence.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then it wouldn’t be news,” Kairavi said.<\/p>\n<p>Meera appeared at the kitchen door, wiping her hands on her <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"kurta\">kurta<\/span>. “You have a fever.”<\/p>\n<p>“I have just entered the house.”<\/p>\n<p>“Your face.”<\/p>\n<p>“My face is tired.”<\/p>\n<p>Meera put a palm against her forehead before Kairavi could object. “Warm.”<\/p>\n<p>“The office was freezing. My body is confused.”<\/p>\n<p>“Change your clothes. I’ll make tea.”<\/p>\n<p>On the dining table lay a small cardboard box with a picture of wireless headphones on it.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi recognised the model. She had paused beside it at the supermarket two weeks ago, checked the price and put it back because her old pair still worked if she bent the cable near the plug.<\/p>\n<p>“What is this?” she asked.<\/p>\n<p>“Your father brought it,” Meera said.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi touched the box. “Why?”<\/p>\n<p>“Your old one is broken.”<\/p>\n<p>“It works.”<\/p>\n<p>“If you have to hold the wire with your teeth, it is broken.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi almost smiled. Then she remembered the phone call and removed her hand from the box.<\/p>\n<p>Aarohi came out carrying a laptop covered in stickers. At nineteen, she possessed the confidence of someone who had not yet been given enough official reasons to doubt herself.<\/p>\n<p>“You’re late,” she said.<\/p>\n<p>“Apparently the family has appointed a timekeeping committee.”<\/p>\n<p>“I need help.”<\/p>\n<p>“That part I heard.”<\/p>\n<p>They sat together at the dining table. Aarohi had opened six browser tabs: three university programmes, two online courses and a video titled <strong>Machine Learning Roadmap — Zero to Expert in 90 Days<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi closed the video.<\/p>\n<p>“First lesson,” she said. “Anyone promising to make you an expert in ninety days is selling you the ninety days.”<\/p>\n<p>“So which course?”<\/p>\n<p>“None, until you decide whether you actually enjoy the work.”<\/p>\n<p>“I do.”<\/p>\n<p>“You enjoy using apps.”<\/p>\n<p>“I made a recommendation system.”<\/p>\n<p>“You copied one from a tutorial.”<\/p>\n<p>“I understood at least forty per cent.”<\/p>\n<p>“Good. Explain that forty.”<\/p>\n<p>Aarohi groaned, but she began.<\/p>\n<p>Her explanation was clumsy, enthusiastic and occasionally wrong. Kairavi listened without correcting every sentence. She asked questions instead. Why had Aarohi chosen those features? What happened when a new user had no history? How would she know if the recommendations were useful rather than merely familiar?<\/p>\n<p>By the third question, Aarohi had stopped performing confidence and started thinking.<\/p>\n<p>That, Kairavi knew, was where learning actually began.<\/p>\n<p>Rajeev returned shortly after nine. He placed his keys in their usual bowl, inspected the locked balcony door and asked why the television was so loud before sitting at the table.<\/p>\n<p>He was a broad man whose hair had begun retreating from his forehead in a line he refused to acknowledge. He smelled faintly of cardboard cartons, aftershave and the cold storage room at the supermarket.<\/p>\n<p>“Show me,” he said to Aarohi.<\/p>\n<p>She turned the laptop towards him and described the degree programme with more certainty than she had shown while explaining her recommendation system to Kairavi.<\/p>\n<p>Rajeev nodded. “Good field. Stable. Every company needs it now.”<\/p>\n<p>“Every company needed accounting too,” Aarohi said. “You didn’t let me take <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"commerce\">commerce<\/span>.”<\/p>\n<p>“Because you hated accounts.”<\/p>\n<p>“I hate chemistry more.”<\/p>\n<p>“You hate whatever you studied most recently.”<\/p>\n<p>Meera set dinner on the table. “Let her finish.”<\/p>\n<p>Rajeev looked at the university address. “No need to go far for this. There are good colleges in Delhi.”<\/p>\n<p>The sentence entered Kairavi quietly and struck the oldest place it could find.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi placed her glass on the table. “<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"papa\">Papa<\/span>, before Aarohi applies, please ask her what she wants. Not only where you would prefer her to study.”<\/p>\n<p>Rajeev looked up. “I didn’t say that.”<\/p>\n<p>“You mentioned Delhi before asking whether any of these programmes interested her.”<\/p>\n<p>Aarohi lowered the laptop screen by an inch. Meera set the serving spoon down carefully.<\/p>\n<p>“We are discussing her course,” Rajeev said.<\/p>\n<p>“I know. We had the same conversation about mine.”<\/p>\n<p>“Kairavi,” Meera warned.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi looked at her mother. “I’m not trying to start a fight, <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"maa\">Maa<\/span>. I don’t want Aarohi’s options decided before she has even understood them.”<\/p>\n<p>Rajeev leaned back. “You have a good job. You work for an international company five kilometres from this house. What exactly was ruined?”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi felt the answer rise so quickly that it frightened her.<\/p>\n<p>The observatory she had never entered. The university acceptance she had read until the words blurred. The nights spent learning constellations from a roof crowded with <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"water-tanks\">water tanks<\/span>. The application essay in which she had written that discovery began where certainty ended.<\/p>\n<p>A life could be lost without being ruined. That was what made explaining it so difficult.<\/p>\n<p>“My life was not ruined,” she said. “I enjoy my work, and I am grateful for what I have. But that does not mean I stopped wanting the future I had chosen. Both things can be true.”<\/p>\n<p>Rajeev’s expression tightened. “We made the decision we thought was safe.”<\/p>\n<p>“I understand that you were afraid for me. But it was still a decision you made for me.”<\/p>\n<p>“You were twenty.”<\/p>\n<p>“I was old enough to attend the university and apply for the visa. I needed my family to trust that I was old enough to decide.”<\/p>\n<p>Meera sat beside her. “I supported you.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi turned to her. “You believed I was capable, <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"maa\">Maa<\/span>. I know that. But when believing in me required trusting me to leave, you agreed that I should stay.”<\/p>\n<p>Her mother’s face changed.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi hated that the truth had hurt her. She did not withdraw it.<\/p>\n<p>Aarohi looked from one parent to the other. “<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"didi\">Didi<\/span>, I don’t have to apply now?”<\/p>\n<p>“No.” Kairavi softened her voice. “You do. And if this is what you want, I’ll help you. Even if the best course is somewhere else.”<\/p>\n<p>“No one is stopping her,” Rajeev said.<\/p>\n<p>“Good. Then let’s agree now that the final choice will be hers.”<\/p>\n<p>He pushed his chair back. “You think I enjoy saying no to my children? You think money appears because a brochure has a good photograph? Foreign fees, rent, flights—”<\/p>\n<p>“I had a scholarship.”<\/p>\n<p>“Partial.”<\/p>\n<p>“I had a plan for the rest.”<\/p>\n<p>“A plan is not security.”<\/p>\n<p>“No plan is completely safe,” Kairavi said. “Keeping us nearby may make you feel safer, but it cannot be the condition for having your support.”<\/p>\n<p>Silence spread across the table.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi looked at the unopened headphones. Rajeev followed her eyes.<\/p>\n<p>For a moment, neither of them spoke.<\/p>\n<p>Then he picked up his plate and said, “Transfer ten thousand. I’ll manage the rest.”<\/p>\n<p>It was his version of surrender: five thousand <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"rupee\">rupees<\/span> and no admission that a battle had occurred.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi hated how well she understood him.<\/p>\n<p>“I can transfer ten this time,” she said. “But next month, we decide the household contribution in advance. Please don’t commit my salary before asking me.”<\/p>\n<p>Rajeev did not answer. He also did not argue.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi transferred the money.<\/p>\n<p>At eleven forty, the fever returned.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi lay beneath a thin sheet, wearing the new headphones. The sound was clear and full, much better than the old pair’s. That irritated her too. Rajeev had remembered the exact model.<\/p>\n<p>Her body ached as though she had exercised after months of inactivity. Sleep would not come. Every few minutes she became aware of her own breathing and had to resist the urge to draw a deeper breath.<\/p>\n<p>Outside, the neighbourhood had gone quiet except for an occasional vehicle. Inside, there was only the ceiling fan’s faint click.<\/p>\n<p>She opened her laptop.<\/p>\n<p>The seismic anomaly had reached the international news. Recordings had now been confirmed at stations in India, Indonesia, Kenya, Chile and Australia. Experts agreed on three things: the signatures were real, they did not match any recorded earthquake and there was no common epicentre.<\/p>\n<p>Beyond that, every article became cautious.<\/p>\n<p>Unusual atmospheric activity.<\/p>\n<p>Undocumented industrial interference.<\/p>\n<p>Calibration failure.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi downloaded the publicly available readings from four monitoring stations. Two also provided delayed public live feeds, intended for researchers and classrooms rather than emergency work. The recordings were incomplete and used different sampling rates. She converted the timestamps, aligned the peaks and discarded two hours of work after realising she had mishandled one station’s time zone.<\/p>\n<p>At one thirty-seven in the morning, she began again.<\/p>\n<p>The waveforms were not identical. News graphics had simplified them until they looked that way. The Indian station showed a longer tail. The Chilean reading carried a second low-frequency band. Kenya had registered the event four seconds before Australia, as though it lay closer to a source that did not exist.<\/p>\n<p>But inside each recording, a cluster of narrow pulses repeated.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi marked the distance between them.<\/p>\n<p>Eleven minutes, forty-three seconds.<\/p>\n<p>Eleven minutes, forty-three seconds.<\/p>\n<p>Eleven minutes, thirty-eight seconds.<\/p>\n<p>Eleven minutes, twenty-six seconds.<\/p>\n<p>She sat straighter.<\/p>\n<p>The first two equal gaps were why the news had called it periodic. The later pulses were being treated as measurement noise.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi plotted the intervals.<\/p>\n<p>They were shrinking.<\/p>\n<p>Not smoothly. Not enough for a clean equation. But the deviation moved in one direction across every station.<\/p>\n<p>Her headache sharpened. A dull warmth gathered behind her eyes, then travelled down her throat and settled beneath her ribs.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi pressed both hands against the edge of the desk.<\/p>\n<p>For several seconds, the lines on the graph seemed brighter than the screen that contained them. She saw relationships before she could explain them—distance, sequence, pressure, an approaching point where the spaces between signals would become too small to measure.<\/p>\n<p>Then the sensation vanished.<\/p>\n<p>The room returned: laptop, desk, headphones, darkness.<\/p>\n<p>She checked her temperature.<\/p>\n<p>One hundred point four degrees Fahrenheit.<\/p>\n<p>“Great,” she whispered.<\/p>\n<p>Fever explained the brightness. Exhaustion explained the rest.<\/p>\n<p>It did not explain the data.<\/p>\n<p>Using the last few intervals, she estimated when the next pulse should appear. The range was wide enough to be embarrassing: between two eighteen and two twenty-six.<\/p>\n<p>She set an alert, closed her eyes and listened through the headphones to one of the delayed public feeds translated into sound.<\/p>\n<p>Static.<\/p>\n<p>A faint hiss.<\/p>\n<p>The slow electrical murmur of an instrument listening to a planet that was supposed to be still.<\/p>\n<p>At twelve seconds past two fourteen, a deep tone passed through the feed.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi opened her eyes.<\/p>\n<p>The new spike climbed across the screen.<\/p>\n<p>It had arrived almost four minutes before the beginning of her predicted range.<\/p>\n<p>She added the timestamp to the plot. The curve bent more sharply downward.<\/p>\n<p>From the next room came the sound of Aarohi turning in bed. Somewhere below, a dog began barking. Then another answered from farther away.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi looked towards the closed window.<\/p>\n<p>The glass was trembling.<\/p>\n<p>Not enough to rattle. Barely enough to see. A fine vibration that stopped the moment she stood.<\/p>\n<p>She remained still, one hand on the desk, waiting for it to begin again.<\/p>\n<p>Nothing moved.<\/p>\n<p>On her screen, the intervals continued their descent.<\/p>\n<p>It was not repeating.<\/p>\n<p>It was accelerating.<\/p>"}, {"n": 3, "title": "Spandan", "deva": "स्पंदन", "gloss": "the pulse", "pov": "Raghav, then Kabir", "teaser": "Dinner at the shop becomes the talk about money the three friends have avoided, until a pulse dims the lights.", "summary": "Raghav loses the flat that his loan to Samyukt delayed. Kabir’s cheap vibration monitor records the pulse pattern on an isolated rig. Dinner at the shop becomes the conversation about money that the three friends have avoided: twelve lakh in debt, and a plan to repay it together. A pulse dims the lights, and Raghav hears Kabir say his name without Kabir speaking.", "words": 3435, "minutes": 15, "release": "2026-10-14", "html": "<p class=\"first\">Eight days had passed since the first unexplained pulse, and Delhi had stopped expecting an immediate explanation.<\/p>\n<p>The apartment had excellent sunlight, terrible bathroom tiles and a balcony just wide enough for Raghav to pretend he would grow plants.<\/p>\n<p>“Herbs,” he said, standing in the doorway. “Maybe tomatoes.”<\/p>\n<p>The broker looked at the empty clay pots on the neighbouring balconies. “Very good sunlight, sir.”<\/p>\n<p>“That is what worries me. Plants become emotionally dependent.”<\/p>\n<p>The broker gave a professional smile. It was the kind reserved for customers who had not yet produced a cheque.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav stepped onto the balcony. Nine floors below, cars moved between the apartment blocks in patient rows. The <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"metro\">metro<\/span> crossed the distance on concrete pillars. Beyond it, <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"dwarka\">Dwarka<\/span> spread through pale towers, rooftop <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"water-tanks\">water tanks<\/span> and small rectangles of green that builders described as <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"forest-facing\">forest-facing<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>He had visited the flat three months earlier, when the walls still smelled of fresh paint and the asking price had seemed negotiable. The second bedroom could become an office. The living room had enough space for his father’s old chess table. The parking space was larger than his current hatchback required and exactly right for the Porsche 911 Carrera he planned to own before his knees became too old to enter it with dignity.<\/p>\n<p>He had gone home that day and built a spreadsheet.<\/p>\n<p>Down payment. Registration. Furnishing. Emergency reserve. Expected salary increments. The amount he could save each month without becoming the sort of person who examined restaurant menus from right to left.<\/p>\n<p>The numbers had worked.<\/p>\n<p>Then Samyukt had called.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav had transferred four <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"lakh\">lakh<\/span> <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"rupee\">rupees<\/span> two days later.<\/p>\n<p>He did not regret it. That was the inconvenient part. Regret would have been simpler than standing inside the life he had postponed and still knowing he would make the same choice again.<\/p>\n<p>“I can arrange the remaining amount in three months,” Raghav said.<\/p>\n<p>The broker’s smile changed shape. “Sir, I told you on the phone. Another family has submitted the <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"booking-amount\">booking amount<\/span>.”<\/p>\n<p>“You also said they were considering it.”<\/p>\n<p>“They confirmed this morning.”<\/p>\n<p>“People should not be allowed to make major financial decisions before breakfast.”<\/p>\n<p>The broker looked at his watch.<\/p>\n<p>“All right,” Raghav said. “If they discover the tiles, call me.”<\/p>\n<p>In the lift, he pressed the ground-floor button twice. It ignored him both times and stopped on the seventh floor to admit a woman holding a sleepy toddler.<\/p>\n<p>The child stared at him.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav crossed his eyes.<\/p>\n<p>The child hid a smile against the woman’s shoulder.<\/p>\n<p>By the time the doors opened in the lobby, Raghav was smiling too.<\/p>\n<p>His phone rang as he walked towards the parking area.<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"kaisa-laga\">Kaisa laga<\/span>?” his father asked.<\/p>\n<p>“Bathroom tiles look like someone lost an argument with the colour brown.”<\/p>\n<p>“And the flat?”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav unlocked his car. “Booked.”<\/p>\n<p>There was a pause. “By you?”<\/p>\n<p>“By someone with faster banking reflexes.”<\/p>\n<p>“I told you I could arrange some money.”<\/p>\n<p>“And I told you to keep your retirement money where it is.”<\/p>\n<p>“It is not useful sitting in an account.”<\/p>\n<p>“It is extremely useful. It sits there and prevents me from developing blood pressure.”<\/p>\n<p>His father sighed. “There will be another flat.”<\/p>\n<p>“With better tiles.”<\/p>\n<p>“Come for dinner.”<\/p>\n<p>“Saturday.”<\/p>\n<p>“You said that last Saturday.”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav opened his mouth with a joke prepared. Something about being in high demand. Something easy.<\/p>\n<p>“Tomorrow,” he said instead. “I’ll come tomorrow.”<\/p>\n<p>“Good.”<\/p>\n<p>The pleasure in his father’s voice was quiet enough to hurt.<\/p>\n<p>After his mother left, it had been just the two of them for years. His father rarely asked twice for anything. Raghav had learned to fill the silence before either of them had to admit it was there.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav ended the call and sat behind the wheel without starting the engine.<\/p>\n<p>The balcony had not mattered. The apartment had not been unique. Delhi was full of buildings designed by people who believed beige was a personality.<\/p>\n<p>Still, for a few minutes, he allowed himself to be disappointed.<\/p>\n<p>Then he opened the group chat.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Raghav:<\/strong> Lost the flat.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir replied first.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Kabir:<\/strong> Someone bought it?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Raghav:<\/strong> No. It escaped after seeing my finances.<\/p>\n<p>Three dots appeared beside Samyukt’s name, disappeared and returned.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Samyukt:<\/strong> I’m sorry.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav looked at the two words.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Raghav:<\/strong> Good. As punishment you are buying dinner.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Samyukt:<\/strong> Done.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Kabir:<\/strong> With what money?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Raghav:<\/strong> Excellent point. Kabir is buying dinner.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Kabir:<\/strong> I’m leaving this group.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav started the car.<\/p>\n<p>By ten thirty, he was in a conference room explaining to six senior managers why their software migration plan would fail.<\/p>\n<p>He enjoyed this part of his job more than he admitted. A process map was a chessboard whose pieces complained in meetings. Every department protected its own square. Finance wanted fewer exceptions. Sales wanted every customer to be an exception. Technology wanted requirements that remained unchanged for more than forty-eight hours.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav stood beside the screen and moved three tasks on the timeline.<\/p>\n<p>“If training begins here,” he said, “operations will learn the new system before the final data fields are approved. Half the screenshots in the manual will be wrong by launch.”<\/p>\n<p>The project manager frowned. “We cannot move the launch date.”<\/p>\n<p>“I didn’t move it.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then where do you recover the time?”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav highlighted two approval stages. “These happen in sequence because the process document says they happen in sequence. The teams do not depend on each other. Run them together.”<\/p>\n<p>“Compliance will object.”<\/p>\n<p>“Compliance objected last week because nobody invited them until the end. Invite them now.”<\/p>\n<p>“And if they reject the data structure?”<\/p>\n<p>“Then we discover it on Thursday instead of next month.”<\/p>\n<p>Silence settled over the table.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav had learned that people often called a problem complicated when what they meant was that solving it required two departments to speak to each other.<\/p>\n<p>The project manager studied the revised plan. “This might work.”<\/p>\n<p>“That is the nicest thing anyone has said to me today.”<\/p>\n<p>The meeting ended twelve minutes early. Two managers thanked him. One asked him to send the revised process map. The project manager presented the new schedule to the programme director that afternoon as something the team had developed together.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav watched from the back of the room and made a note in the file where he kept ideas for his future company.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Credit should be assigned clearly. Good people leave when their work becomes ‘ours’ only after it succeeds.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Below it were notes on pricing, client discovery, recruitment and the kind of office he wanted. No grey cubicles. No motivational quotes written by people who underpaid their staff. A coffee machine capable of producing coffee rather than brown regret.<\/p>\n<p>He had a name for the company too.<\/p>\n<p>He had not told anyone. Saying it aloud would make it something that could fail.<\/p>\n<p>At six, Raghav drove to Kabir’s office.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir was waiting in the electronics lab with a circuit board in one hand and the expression of a man whose machine had betrayed him publicly. Rachna stood beside him, holding a tablet.<\/p>\n<p>She wore her hair in a loose braid and had a small burn mark on the cuff of her shirt. Raghav had known her for almost two years and trusted her largely because she disagreed with Kabir without treating disagreement as a defect in his personality.<\/p>\n<p>“He has killed it,” she said as Raghav entered.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir did not look up. “It is not dead.”<\/p>\n<p>On the workbench, a red indicator on a compact sensor unit blinked irregularly. Wires ran from it to a small electric motor.<\/p>\n<p>“What was it before the tragedy?” Raghav asked.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir adjusted a connector. “A vibration-monitoring module.”<\/p>\n<p>“It listens to motors?”<\/p>\n<p>“It measures vibration signatures and temperature drift. If the bearing starts failing, the pattern changes before the motor stops.”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav leaned closer. “Predictive maintenance.”<\/p>\n<p>“A cheaper version. The commercial units cost too much for small factories.”<\/p>\n<p>Rachna handed him the tablet. A graph displayed several coloured lines and a sudden rise near the end.<\/p>\n<p>“It detected the test fault,” she said. “Then it began producing this.”<\/p>\n<p>The final section dissolved into dense, evenly spaced spikes.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav pointed. “That looks familiar.”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir finally glanced at him. “The seismic readings.”<\/p>\n<p>For the past eight days, news channels had shown variations of the same pattern. A pulse without an earthquake. A vibration recorded across continents without a source anyone could locate.<\/p>\n<p>“Interference?” Raghav asked.<\/p>\n<p>“It shouldn’t be. The motor was off and the test rig was isolated. Local mechanical noise would not produce the same clean repetition across three runs.”<\/p>\n<p>“Did you reproduce it?”<\/p>\n<p>“Three times.”<\/p>\n<p>Rachna folded her arms. “Which is why I told him to include it in tomorrow’s demonstration.”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir shook his head. “I don’t know what caused it.”<\/p>\n<p>“You can say that,” she replied.<\/p>\n<p>“In front of the review panel?”<\/p>\n<p>“Engineers are allowed to encounter things they don’t understand.”<\/p>\n<p>“Not during funding reviews.”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav scrolled through the results. The system was clever. More importantly, it solved a problem that cost small manufacturers real money.<\/p>\n<p>“Why haven’t you shown this to your manager before?” he asked.<\/p>\n<p>“It wasn’t ready.”<\/p>\n<p>“What was missing?”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir began listing problems. Battery consumption. False warnings. Sensor placement. Variations across different motors. Each concern was valid. Together, they built a wall tall enough to hide behind.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav placed the tablet on the bench.<\/p>\n<p>“A prototype’s job is to be ugly before people pay you to make it handsome.”<\/p>\n<p>Rachna nodded. “I am stealing that.”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir looked offended by both the metaphor and the alliance.<\/p>\n<p>“Show the working detection,” Raghav continued. “Explain the limitations before they discover them. Keep the unexplained pulses in the appendix, because if you hide them and they appear during the demonstration, you will look dishonest instead of cautious.”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir looked at the board again. “The enclosure is also temporary.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then do not invite the panel to sit on it.”<\/p>\n<p>Rachna laughed.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir tried not to. His mouth failed first.<\/p>\n<p>“You are both irritating,” he said.<\/p>\n<p>“And yet essential,” Raghav replied.<\/p>\n<p>They left the office twenty minutes later. Rachna had dinner plans with her sister, so Raghav and Kabir drove towards Samyukt’s shop.<\/p>\n<p>The evening sky had the colour of unpolished steel. The air felt thick despite the traffic moving through it. Raghav lowered the car window and received heat, dust and three simultaneous horns.<\/p>\n<p>He closed it again.<\/p>\n<p>“Have you been sleeping properly?” Kabir asked.<\/p>\n<p>“Define properly.”<\/p>\n<p>“For enough hours.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then no.”<\/p>\n<p>“Headaches?”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav glanced at him. “Are you selling medicine now?”<\/p>\n<p>“Rachna has had a fever since yesterday. Two people in my office went home with body aches. I have had a headache for three days.”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav flexed his fingers around the steering wheel. That morning, his hands had hurt as though he had spent the night gripping something. He had blamed the laptop, the drive, the apartment or age, in that order.<\/p>\n<p>“Season change,” he said.<\/p>\n<p>“It is September in Delhi. The season changes by becoming a different kind of hot.”<\/p>\n<p>“Pollution.”<\/p>\n<p>“Air quality is better than last week.”<\/p>\n<p>“Work stress.”<\/p>\n<p>“You finished a meeting early today. You sent a message to celebrate.”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav looked at him. “Do you maintain a database of my excuses?”<\/p>\n<p>“Someone has to.”<\/p>\n<p>They found Samyukt at his desk with an untouched cup of tea and three calculators in front of him: a handheld one, one open on his computer and another on his phone. A stack of invoices sat beside him.<\/p>\n<p>“Good,” Raghav said. “You have formed a calculator committee.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt looked up. The shadows beneath his eyes had deepened since Monday. “One of them might give a different answer.”<\/p>\n<p>“That is how accounting fraud begins.”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir picked up the tea. “Cold.”<\/p>\n<p>“Observant.”<\/p>\n<p>“When did you eat?”<\/p>\n<p>“I had lunch.”<\/p>\n<p>“When?”<\/p>\n<p>“During lunchtime.”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav dropped into the chair opposite him. “He has become evasive. Interrogation begins now.”<\/p>\n<p>They ordered <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"rolls\">rolls<\/span> from the stall across the market. Samyukt protested about the price until Raghav reminded him that dinner had been imposed as punishment. When the food arrived, they ate at the desk among the invoices and chemical catalogues.<\/p>\n<p>For ten minutes, conversation remained safely elsewhere. Kabir described his sensor. Raghav described the lost apartment as an architectural escape. Samyukt listened, laughed at the correct places and offered Kabir three useful questions about selling the device to small factories.<\/p>\n<p>He was very good at solving problems that belonged to other people.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav wiped his hands and placed the paper napkin beside the empty wrapper.<\/p>\n<p>“Exactly how much do you owe?”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt’s expression did not change. Only his shoulders became still.<\/p>\n<p>“We have discussed this.”<\/p>\n<p>“We have discussed several smaller numbers that become a larger number when they meet in private.”<\/p>\n<p>“I told you about Sethi.”<\/p>\n<p>“You told us he wanted another payment. You did not tell us the hotel invoice had been delayed again.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt looked at Kabir. “How do you know that?”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir nodded towards the papers. The hotel’s name was printed across the top of the invoice.<\/p>\n<p>“That could be anything,” Samyukt said.<\/p>\n<p>“It says overdue,” Kabir replied.<\/p>\n<p>“It could be an overdue invitation.”<\/p>\n<p>Neither of them smiled.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt leaned back. “The payment will clear on Friday.”<\/p>\n<p>“And Sethi?” Raghav asked.<\/p>\n<p>“Handled.”<\/p>\n<p>“That word has stopped meaning anything when you say it.”<\/p>\n<p>Something flickered across Samyukt’s face. Anger, perhaps, or shame wearing anger’s clothes.<\/p>\n<p>“What do you want me to do?” he asked. “Give you a daily report?”<\/p>\n<p>“A truthful one would be a historic start.”<\/p>\n<p>“Raghav,” Kabir said quietly.<\/p>\n<p>The warning was deserved. Raghav heard the sharpness in his own voice and lowered it.<\/p>\n<p>“I don’t want a report,” he said. “I want to know when my friend is drowning before he asks me to lend him a boat.”<\/p>\n<p>“I asked you for money because there was no other option.”<\/p>\n<p>“That is exactly the problem.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt’s jaw tightened. “You want me to apologise for taking it?”<\/p>\n<p>“No.”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav answered too quickly, because that part was easy.<\/p>\n<p>“I chose to help you. I would choose it again. But you call us when the problem needs money, then disappear when it needs people.”<\/p>\n<p>The market noise moved beyond the glass door. A scooter passed. Someone argued over parking. Inside the shop, the ceiling fan clicked once on every rotation.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir looked at Samyukt. “You spent years telling me that I could say what I was thinking without everyone judging me. Now you decide for us what we are allowed to know.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt looked away.<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"maa\">Maa<\/span> and Ishita don’t know the full amount,” he said.<\/p>\n<p>“We are not asking you to frighten them,” Kabir replied. “We are asking you to stop treating secrecy like protection.”<\/p>\n<p>“If I tell everyone every time something goes wrong, what changes?”<\/p>\n<p>“You stop being alone in the room,” Raghav said.<\/p>\n<p>The answer escaped before he could make it sound lighter.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt looked at him then—not at the joke waiting to be made, not at the friend who always found a plan, but at the bare sentence underneath.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav reached for the last roll.<\/p>\n<p>“Also, someone stops you from making stupid decisions,” he said. “Shared responsibility. Very modern.”<\/p>\n<p>The moment closed, but not completely.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt pushed the roll towards him. “Twelve.”<\/p>\n<p>“Twelve what?”<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"lakh\">Lakh<\/span>. Approximately. Four to you, three to Kabir, five connected to Sethi. The interest and payments make the last figure difficult to calculate.”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav had known most of it. Hearing the total still changed the air.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir folded his arms. “Does Sethi have anything in writing?”<\/p>\n<p>“My father signed the original loan papers. I need to check what is enforceable now.”<\/p>\n<p>“Good,” Raghav said. “We check. We map every payment, every document and every due date. Then we make a plan based on numbers instead of whichever creditor called last.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt rubbed his forehead. “Can this wait until the hotel payment comes?”<\/p>\n<p>“No.”<\/p>\n<p>“Raghav—”<\/p>\n<p>“You told us the number. That was the difficult part. Now let people be useful.”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir opened his laptop without asking.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt stared at both of them, then gave a tired laugh. “You came here for free dinner and turned it into an audit.”<\/p>\n<p>“Nothing in life is free,” Raghav said. “Especially Kabir’s dinner.”<\/p>\n<p>The television mounted above the storage shelves changed from a cricket discussion to a live scientific briefing.<\/p>\n<p>Behind the speaker stood a screen displaying the now-familiar seismic pattern.<\/p>\n<p>“Increase the volume,” Kabir said.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt found the remote beneath a ledger.<\/p>\n<p>“…confirmed that the recurring signals are increasing in frequency,” the scientist said. “Current models indicate that the pattern may reach an unknown critical threshold within three to four months. We emphasise that this does not constitute an earthquake prediction.”<\/p>\n<p>A reporter raised her hand. “What happens at that threshold?”<\/p>\n<p>“We do not yet know that a physical event will occur.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then what exactly are you predicting?”<\/p>\n<p>The scientist paused.<\/p>\n<p>“The continuation of the observed trend.”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav leaned back. “That is an expensive way of saying they don’t know.”<\/p>\n<p>“They cannot invent an answer,” Kabir said.<\/p>\n<p>“News channels do it every evening.”<\/p>\n<p>The reporter asked whether the signals could be caused by secret weapons testing. Another asked about extraterrestrial activity. The scientist developed the expression of someone reconsidering every decision that had led her to a microphone.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt turned the volume down.<\/p>\n<p>“Three months,” he said.<\/p>\n<p>“Maybe four,” Raghav replied. “Plenty of time for the government to form a committee that forms another committee.”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir was looking at his phone. “The newest interval was shorter than their published model predicted.”<\/p>\n<p>“By how much?”<\/p>\n<p>“I don’t know. The raw data isn’t updated.”<\/p>\n<p>The shop lights dimmed.<\/p>\n<p>All three men looked up.<\/p>\n<p>The ceiling fan slowed, but the television remained on. A low vibration passed through the metal shutter. It was not loud enough to be called a sound. Raghav felt it in his molars and along the bones of his hands.<\/p>\n<p>Outside, horns stopped one after another.<\/p>\n<p>For three seconds, the market became unnaturally quiet.<\/p>\n<p>Then every dog within hearing began to bark.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt gripped the edge of the desk. His face had lost colour.<\/p>\n<p>“What happened?” Kabir asked.<\/p>\n<p>“Heat,” Samyukt said through his teeth.<\/p>\n<p>“Where?”<\/p>\n<p>He pressed a palm beneath his ribs. “Here.”<\/p>\n<p>The vibration deepened.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav heard the market return all at once: voices, feet, engines, a child crying near the food stall, someone shouting for an electrician. Within the noise, individual voices separated with impossible clarity.<\/p>\n<p>Move.<\/p>\n<p>Wait.<\/p>\n<p>Where are you?<\/p>\n<p>The words had been spoken somewhere in the market, but he knew the direction of each voice before he found its speaker. For one impossible moment, the people around him felt as clearly positioned as pieces arranged across a chessboard.<\/p>\n<p>Then the lights brightened and the sensation vanished.<\/p>\n<p>The fan resumed its clicking rotation.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir had gone to the front of the shop. “No earthquake.”<\/p>\n<p>The hanging sample bottles were still. Nothing on the shelves had moved.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt took a slow breath. Colour returned gradually to his face.<\/p>\n<p>“We should go,” Kabir said.<\/p>\n<p>They closed the shop together. Samyukt insisted he was well enough to ride home. Kabir inspected him with open disbelief, but after ten minutes the heat had passed and Samyukt refused every alternative.<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"message-when-you-reach\">Message when you reach<\/span>,” Raghav told him.<\/p>\n<p>“Yes, <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"maa\">Maa<\/span>.”<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"maa\">Maa<\/span> would make you leave the bike here.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt put on his helmet. “Which is why we are not telling her.”<\/p>\n<p>“Excellent. Our first lesson about openness has transformed him.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt raised one hand and rode away.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav and Kabir walked towards the parking area. The market had recovered with remarkable speed. Shopkeepers blamed the power grid. Customers resumed bargaining. A man outside the pharmacy replayed a video of the lights dimming, although the recording showed almost nothing.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir carried the vibration module in his backpack. As they reached Raghav’s car, Kabir stopped and opened the backpack’s front pocket.<\/p>\n<p>“The board is active,” he said.<\/p>\n<p>“You left it on?”<\/p>\n<p>“There is no battery.”<\/p>\n<p>He pulled the module out. A red indicator glowed faintly beneath the translucent casing.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav leaned closer.<\/p>\n<p>The light went dark.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir opened the battery compartment. Empty.<\/p>\n<p>Neither man spoke for several seconds.<\/p>\n<p>“Stored charge?” Raghav suggested.<\/p>\n<p>“Not for this long.”<\/p>\n<p>“Faulty capacitor?”<\/p>\n<p>“Maybe.”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir did not sound convinced.<\/p>\n<p>He placed the device carefully inside his bag. Raghav unlocked the car and walked around to the driver’s side.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Raghav.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>He turned.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir stood near the bonnet, looking down at his phone. His mouth had not moved.<\/p>\n<p>“What?” Raghav asked.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir looked up. “I didn’t say anything.”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav studied his face. “You called me.”<\/p>\n<p>“No.”<\/p>\n<p>The pressure in the air returned for a single heartbeat.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir’s expression changed.<\/p>\n<p>“Raghav,” he said in the same tone Raghav had heard a moment earlier. “Something is wrong.”<\/p>"}, {"n": 4, "title": "Aahvan", "deva": "आह्वान", "gloss": "the summons", "pov": "Samyukt", "teaser": "Twelve days of fever and normal test results, then a dream of an ash plain under a fractured sky.", "summary": "Twelve days of fever and normal test results. Ishita finds his old recurve bow, and at the range one shot buries an arrow to the fletching. That night he dreams of an ash plain under a fractured sky, a bow rising from the ash, and a voice asking what he is willing to destroy to protect what he loves.", "words": 3230, "minutes": 14, "release": "2026-10-14", "html": "<p class=\"first\">The doctor told Samyukt to rest as if she had prescribed a rare medicine available only in another country.<\/p>\n<p>“How many hours do you sleep?” she asked.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt considered lying. Naina was sitting beside him, which made the attempt pointless.<\/p>\n<p>“Six,” he said.<\/p>\n<p>“Four,” Naina corrected.<\/p>\n<p>“Sometimes six.”<\/p>\n<p>“When?”<\/p>\n<p>“On special occasions.”<\/p>\n<p>The doctor did not smile. She turned back to the report on her screen.<\/p>\n<p>The clinic smelled of antiseptic and the eucalyptus oil someone in the waiting room had applied generously enough to treat the entire building. A faded poster behind the desk explained the symptoms of seasonal influenza. Samyukt had mentally checked off fever, fatigue and body pain, then rejected the diagnosis because the poster looked too cheerful.<\/p>\n<p>For twelve days, his temperature had risen and fallen without settling into a pattern. The ache in his hands had spread to his shoulders and back. Sleep came in short, restless pieces. Sometimes he woke with the certainty that a loud sound had crossed the room, only to find the house silent.<\/p>\n<p>Every test the doctor had ordered was normal.<\/p>\n<p>“Your blood count is fine,” she said. “There is no obvious infection. It may be a mild viral illness combined with exhaustion and dehydration.”<\/p>\n<p>“So I’m healthy?”<\/p>\n<p>“That is not what I said.”<\/p>\n<p>“But it is close.”<\/p>\n<p>Naina looked at the doctor. “He does this.”<\/p>\n<p>“I can see that.”<\/p>\n<p>The doctor added a note to the prescription. “Fluids, regular meals and proper sleep. No strenuous activity for a few days. If the fever rises, the pain worsens or you develop breathing difficulty, come back immediately.”<\/p>\n<p>“How many days should he stay home?” Naina asked.<\/p>\n<p>“At least two.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt sat straighter. “The reports are normal.”<\/p>\n<p>“Your reports do not run your business,” Naina said.<\/p>\n<p>“Exactly.”<\/p>\n<p>“That was not support.”<\/p>\n<p>The doctor placed the prescription between them. “Two days.”<\/p>\n<p>They left the clinic at eleven fifteen. At eleven twenty-three, Samyukt told Naina he needed to stop at the shop for ten minutes.<\/p>\n<p>At eleven forty, she stood inside his office with her arms folded while he answered a supplier’s call.<\/p>\n<p>“I am leaving,” she announced when he finished.<\/p>\n<p>“I’ll come home after checking the dispatch.”<\/p>\n<p>“Ten minutes ended.”<\/p>\n<p>“Business time moves differently.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then your business can explain that to the doctor.”<\/p>\n<p>She took the motorcycle keys from his desk.<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"maa\">Maa<\/span>.”<\/p>\n<p>“Call a cab when you are finished being foolish.”<\/p>\n<p>She left with the keys.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt watched her through the glass door. One of the workers near the packing table suddenly became absorbed in aligning labels.<\/p>\n<p>“Not a word,” Samyukt said.<\/p>\n<p>The man nodded wisely.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt stayed for the rest of the afternoon.<\/p>\n<p>The unexplained seismic pulses had become part of ordinary conversation. Customers discussed them while arguing over prices. Delivery drivers forwarded videos of shaking windows and barking dogs. News channels placed countdown graphics beside experts who repeatedly explained that there was no official countdown.<\/p>\n<p>The government had formed an international research group. Universities had opened their data to one another. Monitoring stations were being recalibrated and new sensors installed near major cities. The official projection remained unchanged: if the trend continued, the unknown threshold might be reached in three to four months.<\/p>\n<p>Meanwhile, clinics across several countries were reporting unexplained fevers, muscle pain and disturbed sleep.<\/p>\n<p>Most doctors blamed anxiety, weather or the tendency of social media to make unrelated symptoms appear connected. The health ministry had found no common pathogen. There was no reason for panic.<\/p>\n<p>The phrase appeared so often that people had begun printing it on T-shirts.<\/p>\n<p>At two, Raghav and Kabir arrived carrying a laptop and enough determination to qualify as an enforcement agency.<\/p>\n<p>“Why are you here?” Samyukt asked.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav looked around. “Interesting. I was about to ask the patient the same question.”<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"maa\">Maa<\/span> called you?”<\/p>\n<p>“She sent a voice note.”<\/p>\n<p>“How long?”<\/p>\n<p>“One minute and forty-eight seconds.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt winced. “That bad?”<\/p>\n<p>“She used my full name.”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir moved the invoices off the spare chair and opened his laptop. “Sit.”<\/p>\n<p>“I am sitting.”<\/p>\n<p>“Continue.”<\/p>\n<p>For the next hour, they worked through the debt.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav had built a spreadsheet with coloured categories, payment priorities and three possible cash-flow plans. Kabir had scanned the original loan documents and arranged every transfer Samyukt could find in date order. The oldest receipts were in Samar’s handwriting. Several payments had been recorded only as interest, and two handwritten additions to the schedule had no signature from Samar at all.<\/p>\n<p>“This is not a legal opinion,” Kabir said, “but you should show it to someone who can give one.”<\/p>\n<p>“A lawyer will cost money.”<\/p>\n<p>“Less than paying an invalid charge for another five years,” Raghav replied.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt looked at the total projected interest and felt the familiar pressure gather behind his eyes.<\/p>\n<p>“Sethi will not like this.”<\/p>\n<p>“We are building a financial plan,” Raghav said. “His happiness is not a column.”<\/p>\n<p>“It should be,” Kabir said. “Set the value to zero.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt laughed despite the headache.<\/p>\n<p>The sound felt unfamiliar inside the office.<\/p>\n<p>Raghav turned the laptop towards him. “Nothing gets paid outside this order unless all three of us discuss it.”<\/p>\n<p>“It is my debt.”<\/p>\n<p>“Correct.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then why do you get a vote?”<\/p>\n<p>“Because seven <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"lakh\">lakh<\/span> of your debt is sitting in these two handsome chairs.”<\/p>\n<p>Kabir glanced at the cracked plastic beneath him. “Speak for yourself.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt looked at the plan again.<\/p>\n<p>His first instinct was to find the flaw, thank them and return to handling it himself. The plan could fail. The hotel could delay payment again. A customer could cancel. Raw-material prices could rise. Depending on another person created one more place where something could go wrong.<\/p>\n<p>It also created one more place where something might hold.<\/p>\n<p>“Fine,” he said. “We discuss it.”<\/p>\n<p>Raghav extended his hand across the desk.<\/p>\n<p>“This is not a business merger,” Samyukt said.<\/p>\n<p>“It could be. Your company has chemicals. Mine will have solutions. Kabir has one blinking box.”<\/p>\n<p>“The box works,” Kabir said.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt shook Raghav’s hand.<\/p>\n<p>The heat beneath his ribs stirred.<\/p>\n<p>He released the grip and pressed his palm against his abdomen.<\/p>\n<p>Kabir noticed. “Again?”<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"acidity\">Acidity<\/span>.”<\/p>\n<p>“Your <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"acidity\">acidity<\/span> reacts to seismic activity?”<\/p>\n<p>“It is very informed.”<\/p>\n<p>Neither friend accepted the answer, but a delivery <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"tempo\">tempo<\/span> arrived before they could continue.<\/p>\n<p>That evening, Samyukt returned home in a cab. Naina did not say she had been right. She did not need to. She placed <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"khichdi\">khichdi<\/span> in front of him and watched him eat.<\/p>\n<p>Ishita had taken over the living-room floor. Books, old files and cardboard boxes surrounded her in uneven territories.<\/p>\n<p>“What happened?” Samyukt asked.<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"maa\">Maa<\/span> said the cupboard smells.”<\/p>\n<p>“The cupboard is older than you. Show respect.”<\/p>\n<p>“The cupboard is a biological event.”<\/p>\n<p>She reached into a long canvas case and lifted out what lay inside.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt stopped eating.<\/p>\n<p>The recurve bow had once been black. Years of use had left pale marks near the grip, and dust had settled into the shallow scratches along its limbs. The string had been removed and coiled in a separate pouch. His arm guard, finger tab, old scorecards and a few medals lay beneath it.<\/p>\n<p>“I thought you sold this,” Ishita said.<\/p>\n<p>“Why would I sell it?”<\/p>\n<p>“Because you haven’t touched it in three years.”<\/p>\n<p>He set his spoon down and took the bow from her.<\/p>\n<p>The weight returned to his hand with unsettling familiarity. His fingers found the grip before he looked at it. For a moment, he was twelve again, standing at the edge of a training range with a borrowed bow too heavy for him and the image of <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"karna\">Karna<\/span>’s chariot fixed inside his head.<\/p>\n<p>He had first discovered the <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"mahabharata\">Mahabharata<\/span> through television, then through every version he could find. <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"karna\">Karna<\/span> had fascinated him: the abandoned child who became a king, the warrior denied recognition, the giver who refused to stop giving even when generosity stripped away his protection.<\/p>\n<p>At twelve, Samyukt had seen only greatness in that endurance.<\/p>\n<p>At twenty-five, he was less certain where greatness ended and tragedy began.<\/p>\n<p>Ishita wiped dust from one of the medals. “You were good.”<\/p>\n<p>“I am still good.”<\/p>\n<p>“Of course. That’s why the bow lives with expired warranties.”<\/p>\n<p>“Archery does not expire.”<\/p>\n<p>“People get rusty.”<\/p>\n<p>“Metal gets rusty.”<\/p>\n<p>“You manufacture chemicals. Fix yourself.”<\/p>\n<p>Naina sat opposite him. “Go on Sunday.”<\/p>\n<p>“Where?”<\/p>\n<p>“The range.”<\/p>\n<p>“The doctor said no strenuous activity.”<\/p>\n<p>Ishita stared at him. “You ignored the doctor for work, but suddenly her word is sacred when someone suggests enjoyment?”<\/p>\n<p>“Archery is not enjoyment. It is discipline.”<\/p>\n<p>“Even better. You desperately need some.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt examined the old string. It would need replacing. The leather on the finger tab had stiffened. Everything could be restored.<\/p>\n<p>That thought produced a feeling he did not trust.<\/p>\n<p>Hope, perhaps. Or grief with better posture.<\/p>\n<p>“Why did you stop?” Ishita asked.<\/p>\n<p>“Work.”<\/p>\n<p>“You worked before you were twenty-two.”<\/p>\n<p>“Not like this.”<\/p>\n<p>“So you stopped making time.”<\/p>\n<p>“There wasn’t time to make.”<\/p>\n<p>Ishita put the medal down. “There is always time for things everyone else needs from you.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt looked at her.<\/p>\n<p>She was not accusing him. That made the sentence harder to resist.<\/p>\n<p>“Sunday,” she said. “I’m coming with you.”<\/p>\n<p>“Why?”<\/p>\n<p>“To protect innocent targets.”<\/p>\n<p>On Sunday morning, the city woke beneath a colourless sky.<\/p>\n<p>The archery range occupied a large sports ground between a school and an unfinished housing project. Samyukt had trained there for years, although the entrance had been repainted and the equipment shed replaced since his last visit. The grass was damp from overnight watering. Targets stood at measured distances, their bright circles almost rude against the grey morning.<\/p>\n<p>The instructor on duty inspected Samyukt’s bow and refused to let him use the old string.<\/p>\n<p>“Limbs are fine,” he said. “String is not. Use this for today.”<\/p>\n<p>He handed over a replacement and watched Samyukt string the bow.<\/p>\n<p>The motion pulled sharply across Samyukt’s shoulders.<\/p>\n<p>He hid the discomfort from Ishita, who was sitting behind the safety line with two cups of tea and the alertness of a person waiting for public failure.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt fastened his arm guard. The familiar sequence returned in pieces: stance, grip, nock, hook, raise, draw.<\/p>\n<p>His first arrow landed outside the blue ring.<\/p>\n<p>Ishita lowered her tea. “Still good?”<\/p>\n<p>“Wind.”<\/p>\n<p>The flags above the targets hung motionless.<\/p>\n<p>“Very selective wind.”<\/p>\n<p>The second arrow struck low. The third landed left.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt felt irritation rise with every shot. His mind knew where his elbow belonged, how the string should settle against his face and how the release should happen without being forced. His body delivered approximations.<\/p>\n<p>By the sixth arrow, his shoulder trembled.<\/p>\n<p>He lowered the bow.<\/p>\n<p>The fever had gone that morning, but the ache remained deep inside his joints. He had expected the discipline to return like a switch being turned on. Instead, every neglected year stood with him at the shooting line.<\/p>\n<p>“Take a break,” Ishita called.<\/p>\n<p>“I just started.”<\/p>\n<p>“Your arm disagrees.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt stepped away from the line and drank water. Around him, younger archers released in steady rhythm. Strings snapped. Arrows struck foam. An instructor corrected someone’s foot position with the patience of a person repeating the same sentence for the thousandth time.<\/p>\n<p>Ishita handed him the tea.<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"karna\">Karna<\/span> would have hit the centre,” she said.<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"karna\">Karna<\/span> did not take a three-year break.”<\/p>\n<p>“He also had <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"divine-armour\">divine armour<\/span>. You have a medical prescription.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt smiled into the cup.<\/p>\n<p>Ishita turned one of his old medals over in her hand. She had brought it without telling him.<\/p>\n<p>“You know what is strange?” she asked. “When you talk about <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"karna\">Karna<\/span>, you never talk about whether he was happy.”<\/p>\n<p>“That is not why people remember him.”<\/p>\n<p>“Exactly.”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt looked towards the targets.<\/p>\n<p>“He remained generous after people humiliated him,” he said. “He kept his word when breaking it would have saved him. He became great without being given the opportunities other people had.”<\/p>\n<p>“I understand why you admire him.” Ishita traced the edge of the medal with her thumb. “I just think you admire what he endured so much that sometimes you confuse enduring everything with doing the right thing.”<\/p>\n<p>The words settled more deeply than Samyukt wanted them to.<\/p>\n<p>“When did a BA in psychology make you an expert on the <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"mahabharata\">Mahabharata<\/span>?”<\/p>\n<p>“It didn’t. Growing up with you made me an expert on you.”<\/p>\n<p>The instructor called the next round.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt returned to the line.<\/p>\n<p>This time, he stopped trying to prove that nothing had changed.<\/p>\n<p>He widened his stance by half an inch, eased the tension in his bow hand and accepted the tremor in his shoulder. He breathed into his abdomen, slowly, until the noise around him separated from the work in front of him.<\/p>\n<p>Raise.<\/p>\n<p>Draw.<\/p>\n<p>Anchor.<\/p>\n<p>Release.<\/p>\n<p>The arrow landed in the red.<\/p>\n<p>The next joined it. The third drifted into blue, but the grouping had tightened.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt continued.<\/p>\n<p>The old satisfaction returned without drama. It was not the pleasure of hitting the centre. It was the relief of discovering that improvement still obeyed effort.<\/p>\n<p>On his final end, he raised the bow and felt heat gather beneath his ribs.<\/p>\n<p>He almost lowered it.<\/p>\n<p>Instead, he held.<\/p>\n<p>The warmth rose through his chest and divided across his shoulders. It travelled down both arms—not painfully, but with the smooth pressure of water finding an old channel.<\/p>\n<p>His fingers stopped trembling.<\/p>\n<p>The bow became light in his hand.<\/p>\n<p>For one breath, the target appeared impossibly close. Samyukt could see a torn fibre beside the yellow circle, the shadow cast by the previous arrow and the exact movement of the paper face in air currents he could not feel.<\/p>\n<p>He released.<\/p>\n<p>The bowstring snapped forward with a sound deeper than it should have made.<\/p>\n<p>The arrow hit near the centre and disappeared into the target up to its fletching. Whatever had moved through him had found an old path, flared once and vanished before he could understand it. When he reached for that impossible steadiness again, he found only the ordinary ache in his shoulders.<\/p>\n<p>Everyone on the line stopped.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt lowered the bow.<\/p>\n<p>The heat vanished. Pain rushed back into his shoulder.<\/p>\n<p>The instructor walked downrange after the signal and examined the target. When he pulled, the arrow refused to move.<\/p>\n<p>“What poundage is this?” he called.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt told him.<\/p>\n<p>The instructor looked at the buried shaft, then back at the bow.<\/p>\n<p>“That is not possible.”<\/p>\n<p>Ishita had stopped smiling.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt flexed his fingers. They felt warm, but otherwise ordinary.<\/p>\n<p>“Damaged foam,” he said.<\/p>\n<p>The instructor moved to the rear of the target and inspected it again.<\/p>\n<p>“Maybe,” he replied, without conviction.<\/p>\n<p>On the ride home, Ishita asked about the shot twice. Samyukt blamed the target twice. The third time, she remained quiet.<\/p>\n<p>By evening, his fever had returned.<\/p>\n<p>He ate dinner in bed under Naina’s supervision and fell asleep before ten. The old bow rested inside its canvas case near the cupboard. His final arrow remained at the range because removing it would have damaged both the shaft and the target.<\/p>\n<p>At some point during the night, the ceiling fan disappeared.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt stood barefoot on a plain covered in ash.<\/p>\n<p>There was no horizon. Grey earth met a black sky without distance between them. Wind moved across the ground in long, pale sheets, lifting ash without touching his clothes.<\/p>\n<p>Above him, the sky was fractured.<\/p>\n<p>The break ran from one end of darkness to the other—a thin wound with no stars behind it. Something pressed against the far side. Not a body. Not a face. A weight too large for either.<\/p>\n<p>A beat travelled through the ground.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt felt it in his teeth.<\/p>\n<p>Another followed.<\/p>\n<p>It was the same pulse the instruments had recorded, slowed until the space between one vibration and the next became enormous.<\/p>\n<p>The third beat carried another sound within it.<\/p>\n<p>A <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"damaru\">damaru<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The rhythm crossed the plain. With every beat, the ash lifted higher.<\/p>\n<p>Far ahead, someone sat in meditation.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt could not judge the distance. The figure seemed both beyond reach and close enough to touch. Matted hair rose above his head. A crescent moon shone against it without illuminating the darkness. Ash covered his skin. At his throat rested a blue so deep it seemed to contain its own night.<\/p>\n<p>A trident stood in the ground beside him.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt knew.<\/p>\n<p>His knees weakened. He lowered his head and brought his palms together, but no prayer came. Every <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"mantra\">mantra<\/span> he had read, every name he knew and every question he had ever imagined vanished beneath the fact of that presence.<\/p>\n<p>Between them, something emerged from the ash.<\/p>\n<p>A bow.<\/p>\n<p>It was taller than the one Samyukt had used that morning. Its limbs curved like two fragments of darkness drawn into shape. There was no string. Beneath the black surface, thin veins of violet light moved and disappeared.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt approached it.<\/p>\n<p>The sky cracked wider.<\/p>\n<p>From beyond the opening came a distant mass of voices. Screams, prayers, commands and names called by people who expected an answer.<\/p>\n<p>He reached for the bow.<\/p>\n<p>The grip was cold.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt pulled.<\/p>\n<p>It did not move.<\/p>\n<p>He tightened both hands and planted his feet. The ash gave way beneath them. The bow remained fixed in the earth.<\/p>\n<p>The meditating figure opened his eyes.<\/p>\n<p>The plain became still.<\/p>\n<p>The question reached Samyukt without crossing the distance between them.<\/p>\n<p>“What are you willing to destroy in order to protect what you love?”<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt looked towards the broken sky. The voices beyond it grew louder.<\/p>\n<p>He thought of Naina sitting beside him at the clinic. Ishita holding his forgotten medal. Raghav hiding disappointment inside jokes. Kabir opening the loan documents without judgement. His father struggling for breath beside him in the car.<\/p>\n<p>His hands closed around the bow.<\/p>\n<p>“Whatever tries to harm them.”<\/p>\n<p>The answer left him with absolute certainty.<\/p>\n<p>For the first time, something moved in the figure’s expression.<\/p>\n<p>It was not approval.<\/p>\n<p>“That was not the question.”<\/p>\n<p>The words struck harder than the <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"damaru\">damaru<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The fracture tore open.<\/p>\n<p>Darkness poured through it—not empty darkness, but movement layered upon movement. Shapes collided behind a surface thinner than skin. The voices became a single scream.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt pulled again.<\/p>\n<p>The bow rose from the ash.<\/p>\n<p>Violet light raced along its limbs. A string formed between them, narrow and bright enough to cut the night.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt turned towards the opening.<\/p>\n<p>There was no arrow.<\/p>\n<p>He drew anyway.<\/p>\n<p>Something gathered against his fingers. Heat, sound and pressure folded into a single point. The scream beyond the sky answered it.<\/p>\n<p>Before he could release, the meditating figure struck the <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"damaru\">damaru<\/span> once.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt woke sitting upright.<\/p>\n<p>His left arm was extended before him. His right hand was drawn beside his face, fingers closed around a string that was not there.<\/p>\n<p>The room was dark. The fan turned overhead. Sweat had soaked through his shirt, and his heart struck painfully against his ribs.<\/p>\n<p>His phone vibrated on the bedside table.<\/p>\n<p><strong>New anomalous seismic event recorded.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Samyukt lowered his hands.<\/p>\n<p>A news application had attached an audio conversion of the latest pulse. He pressed play.<\/p>\n<p>Static filled the room.<\/p>\n<p>Then came the low mechanical vibration he had heard on television.<\/p>\n<p>Beneath it, almost too deep to separate from his own heartbeat, sounded the rhythm of a <span class=\"w\" data-g=\"damaru\">damaru<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Samyukt looked towards the canvas case near the cupboard.<\/p>\n<p>For the first time, the disturbance did not feel like something approaching.<\/p>\n<p>It felt like something calling him.<\/p>"}, {"n": 5, "title": "Lakshan", "deva": "लक्षण", "gloss": "the sign", "pov": "Kairavi", "teaser": "During a pulse at the office, Kairavi sees faint lines of strain along the glass.", "summary": "In the seventh week the seismologist Dr Leela Menon answers Kairavi’s model and brings her into an emergency review group. During a pulse at the office Kairavi evacuates most of the floor, sees faint lines of strain along the glass, then misjudges a corridor, and a colleague is hurt. Menon calls: seven concentrations, roughly one per continent, and the one over northern India is gathering.", "words": 2726, "minutes": 12, "release": "2026-10-14", "html": "<p class=\"first\">By the seventh week, the world had learned to live between pulses.<\/p>\n<p>News channels no longer interrupted every programme when one occurred. Schools sent children home only if the lights failed for more than ten minutes. Offices added bottled water and torches to emergency cupboards, then returned to arguing about attendance. Videos of trembling windows competed online with recipes, election speeches and dancing dogs.<\/p>\n<p>The extraordinary had not become ordinary.<\/p>\n<p>It had merely acquired a schedule.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi no longer trusted that schedule.<\/p>\n<p>At two thirty-seven on Thursday morning, she sat at her desk with four monitoring feeds open across two screens. A notebook lay beneath her left hand, its pages filled with intervals, corrections and questions she had stopped pretending she would organise later.<\/p>\n<p>The pulses were arriving sooner.<\/p>\n<p>That much had finally been acknowledged by every major research group studying them. The official models accounted for the change and continued to place the unknown critical threshold between three and four months after the first reading.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi’s model did not.<\/p>\n<p>She checked the formula again.<\/p>\n<p>The problem was not that the intervals were shrinking. The problem was that they were shrinking faster with every new cluster.<\/p>\n<p>If a car gained the same amount of speed every minute, its acceleration remained stable. This was different. It was as if the car increased its acceleration every time the road shortened beneath it.<\/p>\n<p>She deleted the comparison from the note she was preparing. A seismologist did not need an AI engineer explaining cars to her.<\/p>\n<p>What a seismologist might need was the graph beneath the note.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi had built three versions. The first assumed that the changes between pulses were measurement noise. It produced the official three-to-four-month range. The second treated the acceleration as stable and reduced the estimate considerably. The third allowed the acceleration itself to increase.<\/p>\n<p>That curve reached the threshold in less than three weeks.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi stared at it until the numbers lost meaning.<\/p>\n<p>She was not a seismologist. The data was incomplete, several stations used incompatible sampling rates and the threshold itself was inferred from a change in waveform behaviour that nobody understood. A beautiful equation built on a wrong assumption remained wrong.<\/p>\n<p>She wrote that sentence at the top of her email.<\/p>\n<p>Then she attached the calculations and sent them to seven research institutions.<\/p>\n<p>By morning, five had produced automated acknowledgements. One address rejected the attachment. The seventh sent nothing.<\/p>\n<p>“You look terrible,” Mitali said when Kairavi reached the office.<\/p>\n<p>“Good morning to you too.”<\/p>\n<p>“Your hair is making independent decisions.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi touched it and discovered that Mitali was correct.<\/p>\n<p>They entered the lift with nine other employees. Someone had stuck a printed notice above the buttons:<\/p>\n<p><strong>IN CASE OF A SEISMIC PULSE, REMAIN CALM. THIS BUILDING IS EARTHQUAKE COMPLIANT.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Mitali read it twice. “Earthquake compliant?”<\/p>\n<p>“It has signed the terms and conditions.”<\/p>\n<p>“Excellent. I feel safe.”<\/p>\n<p>The lift doors closed.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi had slept for ninety minutes. The pressure behind her eyes had become so familiar that she noticed it mainly when it disappeared. Her fever had fallen two days earlier, but a dull ache remained in her wrists and along her spine.<\/p>\n<p>“You were working on the signal again,” Mitali said.<\/p>\n<p>It was not a question.<\/p>\n<p>“I sent the model to a few researchers.”<\/p>\n<p>“At what time?”<\/p>\n<p>“A professional time.”<\/p>\n<p>“Kairavi.”<\/p>\n<p>“Three twelve.”<\/p>\n<p>Mitali waited until the other employees left the lift, then caught the doors before they closed.<\/p>\n<p>“You found a pattern,” she said. “That does not make you responsible for the planet.”<\/p>\n<p>“I know.”<\/p>\n<p>“Do you?”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi stepped out. “No. But it makes me responsible for telling someone who might understand it.”<\/p>\n<p>Mitali followed her onto the floor. “You have told them.”<\/p>\n<p>“I sent seven emails.”<\/p>\n<p>“Then tonight you sleep.”<\/p>\n<p>“That sounded like an order.”<\/p>\n<p>“It was polite but firm.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi looked at her.<\/p>\n<p>Mitali smiled. “I learn from the best.”<\/p>\n<p>At eleven twenty, a reply arrived.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Dr Leela Menon<\/strong> introduced herself as a seismologist working with the international analysis group. Her message was brief and unsentimental.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Your normalisation choices are reasonable. Your projected threshold is not. You are treating observations from separate regions as comparable expressions of one process. Their amplitudes, local geology and instrument conditions differ. A shared interval does not prove a shared source.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Kairavi read it twice.<\/p>\n<p>The criticism was valid.<\/p>\n<p>She had aligned the timestamps because the pulses appeared together. She had then treated differences between stations as distortions around one underlying signal. That was an assumption, not a fact.<\/p>\n<p>Below the criticism, Dr Menon had added another line.<\/p>\n<p><strong>However, your second-order acceleration is present in our internal data. If you are willing to join our emergency external-review group, use the secure link below. It includes a confidentiality agreement. Once access is approved, send the uncompressed model and your uncertainty calculations.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Kairavi forgot her headache.<\/p>\n<p>The approval arrived twenty-three minutes after she signed. For the next two hours, she cleaned the files, documented every transformation and removed three lines of code that worked but would have embarrassed her if another person had seen them.<\/p>\n<p>Mitali watched from the next desk.<\/p>\n<p>“I thought tonight was for sleeping.”<\/p>\n<p>“A scientist replied.”<\/p>\n<p>“Scientists are the reason you need sleep.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi sent the model at one forty-eight.<\/p>\n<p>At one forty-nine, the lights dimmed.<\/p>\n<p>Nobody reacted immediately. Power fluctuations had become common during the pulses, although no one had explained why a seismic event with no earthquake affected electrical systems.<\/p>\n<p>The air-conditioning stopped.<\/p>\n<p>The office fell quiet enough for Kairavi to hear the ventilation fans winding down.<\/p>\n<p>Then the glass partitions began to hum.<\/p>\n<p>The sound moved across the floor from east to west. One panel vibrated, fell silent and passed the note to the next. Coffee rippled inside mugs. Monitors flickered. Somewhere beyond the workstations, a woman cried out.<\/p>\n<p>The pressure behind Kairavi’s eyes sharpened.<\/p>\n<p>“Not the lifts,” she said.<\/p>\n<p>Several people were already moving towards them.<\/p>\n<p>“Use the stairs. The backup supply may not hold.”<\/p>\n<p>Varun emerged from a meeting room with his phone raised. “Building management says remain at our desks.”<\/p>\n<p>The easternmost glass panel bowed in its frame.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi saw the sequence again: vibration, silence, vibration. Each panel responded a fraction later than the one before it.<\/p>\n<p>“They haven’t seen this floor,” she said. “Move away from the glass.”<\/p>\n<p>The first panel cracked.<\/p>\n<p>The sound ended the argument.<\/p>\n<p>People moved at once. Chairs struck desks. Someone knocked over a monitor. The fire alarm began sounding even though there was no fire.<\/p>\n<p>Mitali took charge of the employees near the conference rooms. Varun opened the emergency exit. Kairavi stood where she could see both corridors and directed people towards the eastern stairwell, behind the movement of the vibration rather than ahead of it.<\/p>\n<p>For several seconds, the office changed before her eyes.<\/p>\n<p>Faint lines seemed to appear along the glass frames, ceiling joints and electrical conduits. Some were bright and taut. Others broke into scattered fragments. The people suffering the worst dizziness carried the same faint distortion around their heads and chests.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi blinked.<\/p>\n<p>The lines remained for another breath.<\/p>\n<p>She understood none of them. Only one relationship appeared clear: the pressure had already passed through the eastern corridor, while the western side had not yet taken its full force.<\/p>\n<p>“East stairs,” she repeated. “The west side hasn’t taken it yet. Keep moving.”<\/p>\n<p>The first group reached the stairwell safely.<\/p>\n<p>Another glass panel shattered behind them.<\/p>\n<p>An employee named Dev had collapsed near the pantry. Mitali and two others lifted him under the arms. Kairavi counted the remaining people, checked the meeting rooms and directed a second group towards the same exit.<\/p>\n<p>Then smoke appeared near the eastern door.<\/p>\n<p>The backup-power cabinet had overheated. Dark smoke rolled along the ceiling outside the stairwell, and the emergency light above the door went out.<\/p>\n<p>People stopped.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi looked west.<\/p>\n<p>The bright lines there had faded. The partitions were no longer humming.<\/p>\n<p>She made the decision.<\/p>\n<p>“Use the western stairwell. Stay against the interior wall.”<\/p>\n<p>Varun looked at the ceiling. “You said the pressure hadn’t reached that side.”<\/p>\n<p>“It has passed now.”<\/p>\n<p>That was what the pattern suggested.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi led them through the western corridor.<\/p>\n<p>The first ceiling panel fell three steps ahead of her.<\/p>\n<p>It struck the floor and burst into white fragments. Someone screamed. Kairavi stopped so quickly that Mitali collided with her shoulder.<\/p>\n<p>For an instant, the lines seemed to return above them, brighter than before.<\/p>\n<p>“Back!” Kairavi shouted.<\/p>\n<p>The metal frame holding a suspended light tore loose.<\/p>\n<p>Varun pushed the employee beside him away. The edge of the frame struck his shoulder and glanced across his forehead, knocking him to one knee.<\/p>\n<p>Blood appeared immediately.<\/p>\n<p>For a second, Kairavi could see nothing except the route she had chosen.<\/p>\n<p>Then training, logic and guilt separated into different places.<\/p>\n<p>“Mitali, take everyone back to the central meeting room. Interior walls, away from the ceiling fixtures.”<\/p>\n<p>Mitali looked at Varun.<\/p>\n<p>“I’m staying with him. Go.”<\/p>\n<p>This time, nobody questioned her.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi crouched beside Varun. He was conscious, angry and trying to stand.<\/p>\n<p>“Don’t,” she said.<\/p>\n<p>“We need to move.”<\/p>\n<p>“We will. Let me see your head first.”<\/p>\n<p>The cut was narrow but bleeding heavily. He could still move the shoulder, although every attempt made him wince. Kairavi took a clean cloth from the emergency kit mounted on the wall and pressed it into his hand.<\/p>\n<p>“Hold this here. Firmly.”<\/p>\n<p>The vibration weakened.<\/p>\n<p>Above them, the lines disappeared.<\/p>\n<p>One moment they had filled the corridor. The next, Kairavi was staring at ordinary ceiling panels, metal brackets and cable trays.<\/p>\n<p>She tried to call the lines back. Nothing returned. Whatever had happened was not a tool she could command; it had passed through her with the pressure and gone.<\/p>\n<p>She had no explanation for what she had seen.<\/p>\n<p>She did have an explanation for Varun’s blood.<\/p>\n<p>“I was wrong about this corridor,” she said.<\/p>\n<p>He looked up at her, one hand holding the cloth to his forehead. “You were right about the lifts.”<\/p>\n<p>“Both are true.”<\/p>\n<p>They waited until the safety team reached them. Two lifts had stopped between floors. No one inside was seriously injured, but the passengers remained trapped for nearly forty minutes. Across the building, eleven people were treated for cuts, falls or breathing difficulty. Dev’s fever reached 103°F.<\/p>\n<p>Varun received four stitches and left with his arm in a sling.<\/p>\n<p>By six, the office had closed for inspection.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi stood outside beneath a sky the colour of dirty glass. Employees gathered in small groups, replaying videos and improving their memories of what had happened. Some said the building had twisted. Others claimed the glass had moved like water.<\/p>\n<p>Mitali handed Kairavi a bottle.<\/p>\n<p>“Drink.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi obeyed.<\/p>\n<p>“You got most of us out,” Mitali said.<\/p>\n<p>“I sent them into the western corridor.”<\/p>\n<p>“After the other exit filled with smoke.”<\/p>\n<p>“I thought the pressure had passed.”<\/p>\n<p>“And you were wrong.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi looked at her.<\/p>\n<p>Mitali held her gaze. “You were wrong about one thing and right about several others. If you turn those into one judgement about yourself, you’re doing bad analysis.”<\/p>\n<p>“Varun needed stitches.”<\/p>\n<p>“Yes.”<\/p>\n<p>Mitali did not soften the answer. Kairavi was grateful for that.<\/p>\n<p>“I saw something,” Kairavi said.<\/p>\n<p>“What?”<\/p>\n<p>“Lines. Along the glass and the ceiling. Around some people.”<\/p>\n<p>Mitali glanced at the building. “Like cracks?”<\/p>\n<p>“No. Relationships.”<\/p>\n<p>The word sounded inadequate the moment she used it.<\/p>\n<p>“I don’t know,” Kairavi admitted. “It disappeared when the pulse ended.”<\/p>\n<p>Mitali touched the back of Kairavi’s hand. “Then write down exactly what you remember. After you sleep.”<\/p>\n<p>At home, Kairavi found three bags of rice beside the dining table and Rajeev arguing with a wholesaler on the phone.<\/p>\n<p>“No, not tomorrow,” he said. “Tonight. Water, batteries and every first-aid kit you have. I’ll pay the difference.”<\/p>\n<p>He ended the call and looked at the small bandage on Kairavi’s forearm. She had not noticed the cut until the safety team pointed it out.<\/p>\n<p>“What happened?” he asked.<\/p>\n<p>“A partition broke.”<\/p>\n<p>Meera emerged from the kitchen. “You said you were fine.”<\/p>\n<p>“I am fine.”<\/p>\n<p>“You are bleeding.”<\/p>\n<p>“Technically, I have finished bleeding.”<\/p>\n<p>Meera took her wrist and inspected the bandage anyway.<\/p>\n<p>Rajeev pointed towards the supplies. “You said to prepare.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi looked at him. “You listened?”<\/p>\n<p>“I listen.”<\/p>\n<p>Meera made a sound from the back of her throat.<\/p>\n<p>Rajeev ignored her. “The shop has bottled water, dry food, torches and basic medicines. I’ve ordered more. What else?”<\/p>\n<p>For the first time she could remember, her father was not telling her what the family would do.<\/p>\n<p>He was asking.<\/p>\n<p>“Copies of everyone’s documents,” Kairavi said. “Cash in smaller notes. Power banks charged. Shoes and essential medicines together in one place. We decide where to meet if the phones fail.”<\/p>\n<p>Rajeev nodded and began writing.<\/p>\n<p>“This does not mean something terrible will happen,” she added. “It means we should be able to manage for a few days if services stop.”<\/p>\n<p>“How long?” Aarohi asked.<\/p>\n<p>She stood near the hallway with her laptop held against her chest.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi hesitated. “I don’t know.”<\/p>\n<p>It was not reassuring. It was honest.<\/p>\n<p>“I can help with the data,” Aarohi said. “I can collect reports from different cities and put them into one sheet.”<\/p>\n<p>“No. You need to focus on college.”<\/p>\n<p>“<span class=\"w\" data-g=\"didi\">Didi<\/span>—”<\/p>\n<p>“You don’t know where those files come from. Some of these sites could—”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi stopped.<\/p>\n<p>Rajeev’s pen was still against the paper.<\/p>\n<p>She heard her own argument: danger first, permission never.<\/p>\n<p>Aarohi waited.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi took a breath. “You can help. Use only the sources I send you, and do not download unknown attachments. If you develop a fever, dizziness or body pain, you stop and tell me. That part is not negotiable.”<\/p>\n<p>Aarohi’s expression brightened. “Fine.”<\/p>\n<p>“And you sleep by midnight.”<\/p>\n<p>“That was not part of the deal.”<\/p>\n<p>“It is now.”<\/p>\n<p>Rajeev almost smiled.<\/p>\n<p>Meera did not. She touched Kairavi’s forehead.<\/p>\n<p>“You are warm again.”<\/p>\n<p>“A little.”<\/p>\n<p>“You will eat and sleep before opening that laptop.”<\/p>\n<p>“I need to check whether Dr Menon replied.”<\/p>\n<p>“The earth has survived this long without your email.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi glanced at Rajeev. “Please explain to her that preparation matters.”<\/p>\n<p>“Your mother is correct,” he said.<\/p>\n<p>“Unbelievable.”<\/p>\n<p>She ate. She did not sleep.<\/p>\n<p>At eleven eighteen, after Kairavi’s temporary clearance appeared in the emergency group’s portal, Dr Menon called.<\/p>\n<p>Her voice was lower than Kairavi had expected and carried the clipped exhaustion of someone speaking between emergencies.<\/p>\n<p>“Are you somewhere private?” she asked.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi closed her bedroom door. Aarohi was working at the dining table outside.<\/p>\n<p>“Yes.”<\/p>\n<p>“Your model fails when we combine the amplitudes.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi’s shoulders dropped.<\/p>\n<p>“I expected it might.”<\/p>\n<p>“It succeeds when we separate the regions.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi sat down.<\/p>\n<p>Dr Menon continued. “We kept trying to identify one origin point. There isn’t one. The timings correlate globally, but the pressure signatures are organising locally.”<\/p>\n<p>“How many regions?”<\/p>\n<p>There was a pause.<\/p>\n<p>“Seven concentrations are appearing consistently enough to investigate. The boundaries are enormous. We cannot predict cities, and I will not pretend we can.”<\/p>\n<p>“One per continent?”<\/p>\n<p>“Approximately. Antarctica has the weakest coverage and the worst confidence. Do not turn that into a conclusion.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi opened the encrypted image that had arrived from Dr Menon during the call.<\/p>\n<p>A world map appeared. Seven translucent areas lay across it, each wide enough to cover countries. They were not circles. They bent around coastlines, mountain systems and features Kairavi could not identify.<\/p>\n<p>The Asian region covered much of northern India and the surrounding territory.<\/p>\n<p>At its centre, the newest signals pointed inward.<\/p>\n<p>“What does the pressure represent?” Kairavi asked.<\/p>\n<p>“We do not know.”<\/p>\n<p>“What happens at the threshold?”<\/p>\n<p>“We do not know.”<\/p>\n<p>The honesty frightened her more than speculation would have.<\/p>\n<p>“Will you publish this?”<\/p>\n<p>“After independent verification. An emergency briefing has been called for tomorrow morning.”<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi enlarged northern India. The map dissolved into overlapping uncertainty bands.<\/p>\n<p>“My estimate was sixteen to twenty days.”<\/p>\n<p>Dr Menon did not answer immediately.<\/p>\n<p>Kairavi heard keyboards and distant voices at the other end of the call.<\/p>\n<p>“Your timeline is wrong,” Dr Menon said.<\/p>\n<p>Relief entered Kairavi so quickly that it almost became laughter.<\/p>\n<p>“The latest pulse shifted even our most cautious range forward by six days.”<\/p>\n<p>The relief vanished.<\/p>\n<p>On Kairavi’s screen, the signals surrounding northern India leaned towards one another.<\/p>\n<p>They were not passing beneath the region.<\/p>\n<p>They were gathering there.<\/p>"}], "glossary": [{"id": "ji", "term": "ji", "def": "A Hindi mark of respect added after a name or title, as in ‘Sharma ji’ or ‘Naina ji’.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "haan", "term": "haan", "def": "Hindi for ‘yes’; answering the phone, it means ‘yes, I’m here, go on’.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "maa", "term": "Maa", "def": "Hindi for mother, and the usual way to address one.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "bhai", "term": "bhai", "def": "Hindi for brother; a younger sibling’s everyday way of addressing an older brother, also used between close friends.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "lakh", "term": "lakh", "def": "One hundred thousand. Indians count large sums in lakhs, so ₹5 lakh is five hundred thousand rupees.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "rupee", "term": "rupee (₹)", "def": "India’s currency, written with the symbol ₹.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "indian-digit-grouping", "term": "₹1,16,840", "def": "One lakh, sixteen thousand, eight hundred and forty rupees. Indian notation puts a comma after the thousands, then after every two digits.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "challan", "term": "challan", "def": "Indian English for an official slip that accompanies goods or payments, such as a signed delivery note.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "tempo", "term": "tempo", "def": "A small three- or four-wheeled goods vehicle used for local deliveries in Indian cities.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "aloo-paratha", "term": "aloo paratha", "def": "A griddle-cooked flatbread stuffed with spiced mashed potato; a North Indian breakfast that often ends up as a packed lunch.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "coaching-centres", "term": "coaching centres", "def": "Private tuition centres that prepare students for school, college-entrance and government exams; their banners fill Indian market streets.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "mobile-shops", "term": "mobile shops", "def": "Indian English for small shops that sell and repair mobile phones and top up SIM cards.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "momos", "term": "momos", "def": "Steamed or fried dumplings from the Himalayan region, now one of Delhi’s favourite street foods.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "rajma", "term": "rajma", "def": "Red kidney beans simmered in a spiced onion-and-tomato gravy, usually eaten with rice; a North Indian home favourite.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "ma-entrances", "term": "MA entrances", "def": "Entrance examinations for admission to a master’s degree; each university sets its own, with its own fee.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "television-serial", "term": "television serial", "def": "Indian English for a long-running TV drama, often watched by the whole family in the evening.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "pressure-cooker", "term": "pressure cooker", "def": "Indian kitchens cook lentils and rice in pressure cookers, whose loud steam whistles are a familiar sound in every apartment block.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "neki-kar-dariya-mein-daal", "term": "Neki kar, dariya mein daal", "def": "A Hindi proverb: do good and cast it into the river, meaning do good and expect nothing back.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "dwarka", "term": "Dwarka", "def": "A large planned sub-city in south-west Delhi, built in numbered sectors of apartment blocks, markets and metro stations.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "sir-is-in-a-meeting", "term": "‘Sir is in a meeting’", "def": "Indian office English: staff call a senior person ‘Sir’, so this is the standard polite way of not putting a call through.", "ch": 1}, {"id": "papa", "term": "Papa", "def": "The usual way Indian children address their father, in Hindi and English alike.", "ch": 2}, {"id": "didi", "term": "didi", "def": "Hindi for elder sister; younger siblings use it as a form of address, and it extends politely to older women generally.", "ch": 2}, {"id": "poha", "term": "poha", "def": "Flattened rice cooked with onion, mustard seeds, turmeric and peanuts; a quick, cheap Indian breakfast.", "ch": 2}, {"id": "romanised-hindi", "term": "Romanised Hindi", "def": "Hindi typed in the Latin alphabet, as most people do on their phones, often mixed with English in the same sentence.", "ch": 2}, {"id": "mera-paisa-kat-gaya", "term": "Mera paisa kat gaya but payment nahi hua", "def": "Hindi mixed with English: ‘My money was deducted, but the payment didn’t go through.’", "ch": 2}, {"id": "card-block-karo", "term": "Card block karo someone is using it", "def": "Hindi mixed with English: ‘Block my card, someone is using it.’", "ch": 2}, {"id": "chilli-potatoes", "term": "chilli potatoes", "def": "An Indo-Chinese street dish of crisp fried potato strips tossed in a sticky, spicy chilli and soy sauce.", "ch": 2}, {"id": "metro", "term": "metro", "def": "The Delhi Metro, the city’s rapid-transit railway; through Dwarka its trains run on tracks raised high on concrete pillars.", "ch": 2}, {"id": "billing-counter", "term": "billing counter", "def": "Indian English for a shop’s checkout till.", "ch": 2}, {"id": "carry-bag", "term": "carry bag", "def": "Indian English for a shopping bag; shops charge a few rupees for one at the counter.", "ch": 2}, {"id": "kurta", "term": "kurta", "def": "A long, loose tunic worn over trousers, everyday wear for women and men across South Asia.", "ch": 2}, {"id": "water-tanks", "term": "rooftop water tanks", "def": "Indian buildings store their water in tanks on the roof, so rooftops are crowded with plastic and concrete tanks.", "ch": 2}, {"id": "commerce", "term": "commerce", "def": "In Indian schools, the stream of accounts, business and economics taken in the last two years, alongside the science and arts streams.", "ch": 2}, {"id": "kaisa-laga", "term": "Kaisa laga?", "def": "Hindi: ‘How did you find it?’ or ‘Did you like it?’", "ch": 3}, {"id": "forest-facing", "term": "forest-facing", "def": "Indian property-brochure language for a flat that looks out on a patch of green, however small.", "ch": 3}, {"id": "booking-amount", "term": "booking amount", "def": "In Indian property deals, the deposit paid to reserve a flat before the rest of the price is arranged.", "ch": 3}, {"id": "rolls", "term": "rolls", "def": "Delhi street-food wraps: a paratha rolled around kebab, egg, paneer or vegetables, sold hot from market stalls.", "ch": 3}, {"id": "message-when-you-reach", "term": "‘Message when you reach’", "def": "Indian English uses ‘reach’ on its own to mean ‘get there’, so this means ‘text me when you get home’.", "ch": 3}, {"id": "khichdi", "term": "khichdi", "def": "Rice and lentils cooked together into a soft porridge; the classic Indian food for someone who is unwell.", "ch": 4}, {"id": "acidity", "term": "acidity", "def": "Indian English for heartburn or indigestion, an everyday complaint usually blamed on spicy food or stress.", "ch": 4}, {"id": "mahabharata", "term": "Mahabharata", "def": "One of India’s two great Sanskrit epics, about a war between cousins of one royal family; endlessly retold, including on television.", "ch": 4}, {"id": "karna", "term": "Karna", "def": "A warrior of the Mahabharata, remembered for his generosity and his loyalty.", "ch": 4}, {"id": "divine-armour", "term": "divine armour", "def": "In the Mahabharata, the armour the warrior Karna was born wearing.", "ch": 4}, {"id": "damaru", "term": "damaru", "def": "A small hourglass-shaped hand drum with two heads, played by twisting the wrist; it has deep roots in Hindu ritual.", "ch": 4}, {"id": "mantra", "term": "mantra", "def": "A sacred word, sound or verse, often in Sanskrit, repeated in prayer or meditation.", "ch": 4}], "codex": [{"id": "samar-chemicals", "kind": "place", "name": "Samar Chemicals", "deva": "", "ch": 1, "text": "Samyukt’s shop in a Dwarka market: blue drums, white cans waiting for labels, the smell of disinfectant and leaked lemon fragrance. Near the desk, a framed photograph shows Samar and a nineteen-year-old Samyukt beside its newly painted signboard."}, {"id": "naina", "kind": "person", "name": "Naina", "deva": "", "ch": 1, "text": "Samyukt’s mother, who reminds him that tea is not breakfast and asks about the payment and the landlord; he answers “Friday” and “Handled.” She counts grocery money without letting him see."}, {"id": "ishita", "kind": "person", "name": "Ishita", "deva": "", "ch": 1, "text": "Samyukt’s sister, a psychology graduate with two MA entrance forms closing this week. She is also applying for jobs and does not want him paying for everything."}, {"id": "samar", "kind": "person", "name": "Samar", "deva": "", "ch": 1, "text": "Samyukt’s late father, who built the business from nothing and wrote “Neki kar, dariya mein daal” (do good and cast it into the river) inside his ledger. His last meaningful advice: never leave kindness, empathy and compassion behind."}, {"id": "mr-sethi", "kind": "person", "name": "Mr Sethi", "deva": "", "ch": 1, "text": "The creditor on a five lakh rupee loan that Samar signed, grown through years of interest and missed months. He never raises his voice, keeps returning to Samyukt’s father, and settles on thirty-five thousand rupees by Saturday."}, {"id": "signals-without-earthquake", "kind": "sign", "name": "The signals with no earthquake", "deva": "", "ch": 1, "text": "Signals resembling a low-frequency seismic event, recorded at multiple stations though no earthquake occurred, with no epicentre, surface movement or known tectonic cause. That night Kabir reports another: same pattern, same interval."}, {"id": "heat-beneath-the-ribs", "kind": "sign", "name": "The heat beneath the ribs", "deva": "", "ch": 1, "text": "A dull heat that gathers beneath Samyukt’s ribs on the night he first hears of the signals, gone within a breath. For that moment it feels almost deliberate."}, {"id": "mitali", "kind": "person", "name": "Mitali", "deva": "", "ch": 2, "text": "Kairavi’s friend at the office, a data engineer who wears silver eyeliner before noon and treats every office crisis as a story to improve later. Over chilli potatoes she toasts “being right in rooms where it is inconvenient”."}, {"id": "varun", "kind": "person", "name": "Varun", "deva": "", "ch": 2, "text": "A colleague on Kairavi’s project who questions her rerun of the failed cases. Once she finds the flaw, he approves the fix, and at four he removes “production-ready” from the presentation."}, {"id": "rajeev", "kind": "person", "name": "Rajeev", "deva": "", "ch": 2, "text": "Kairavi’s father, still at the supermarket when he phones about dinner. He asks for fifteen thousand rupees more for the household and settles for ten, and has brought home the exact headphones she put back."}, {"id": "meera", "kind": "person", "name": "Meera", "deva": "", "ch": 2, "text": "Kairavi’s mother, who argues back at television debates from the kitchen and spots Kairavi’s fever from her face. She says she supported Kairavi; Kairavi answers that when it meant trusting her to leave, Meera agreed she should stay."}, {"id": "aarohi", "kind": "person", "name": "Aarohi", "deva": "", "ch": 2, "text": "Kairavi’s sister, nineteen, weighing university programmes and online courses in machine learning. She copied a recommendation system from a tutorial and says she understood “at least forty per cent”; Kairavi makes her explain it."}, {"id": "fever", "kind": "sign", "name": "The fever", "deva": "", "ch": 2, "text": "Kairavi’s fever comes and goes, with an ache from neck to wrists, a headache behind one eye and a pressure on her chest. The night she finds the pulses accelerating, it reaches one hundred point four."}, {"id": "accelerating-pulses", "kind": "sign", "name": "The accelerating pulses", "deva": "", "ch": 2, "text": "Aligning four stations overnight, Kairavi finds the gaps between pulses, first eleven minutes forty-three seconds, shrinking at every station. The next pulse comes almost four minutes before her predicted range, and her window glass trembles."}, {"id": "rachna", "kind": "person", "name": "Rachna", "deva": "", "ch": 3, "text": "Beside Kabir in the electronics lab at his office, with a small burn mark on her cuff. Raghav trusts her because she disagrees with Kabir without treating it as a defect, and she wants the unexplained pulses in tomorrow’s demonstration."}, {"id": "vibration-monitoring-module", "kind": "thing", "name": "The vibration-monitoring module", "deva": "", "ch": 3, "text": "Kabir’s low-cost sensor for small factories, meant to catch a failing motor bearing early. With the motor off on an isolated rig, it recorded the seismic pattern three times; after the shop pulse it glowed with no battery."}, {"id": "debt", "kind": "thing", "name": "The debt", "deva": "", "ch": 3, "text": "About twelve lakh rupees that Samyukt owes: four to Raghav, three to Kabir and five connected to Sethi, a figure interest and part-payments make hard to calculate. Naina and Ishita do not know the full amount."}, {"id": "threshold", "kind": "thing", "name": "The threshold", "deva": "", "ch": 3, "text": "An unknown critical threshold that, a televised briefing says, the increasingly frequent signals may reach within three to four months. It is not an earthquake prediction; the scientist will forecast only “the continuation of the observed trend”."}, {"id": "pulse-at-the-shop", "kind": "sign", "name": "The pulse at the shop", "deva": "", "ch": 3, "text": "At Samar Chemicals the lights dim, the fan slows and a vibration too faint to be called a sound passes through the shutter; horns stop, then every dog barks. There is no earthquake, and nothing on the shelves moves."}, {"id": "voices", "kind": "sign", "name": "The voices", "deva": "", "ch": 3, "text": "During the pulse Raghav hears market voices with impossible clarity, knowing each one’s direction, as if people were pieces on a chessboard. By the car, he hears Kabir say his name; Kabir’s mouth has not moved."}, {"id": "recurve-bow", "kind": "thing", "name": "The recurve bow", "deva": "", "ch": 4, "text": "Samyukt’s old recurve, once black, pale near the grip, found by Ishita with his medals. Untouched for three years, it takes him back to a range at twelve, with the Mahabharata’s Karna in his head."}, {"id": "buried-arrow", "kind": "sign", "name": "The buried arrow", "deva": "", "ch": 4, "text": "On Samyukt’s final end at the range, heat rises from beneath his ribs into his arms, the trembling stops and the target seems impossibly close. The arrow sinks to its fletching; the instructor says that is not possible."}, {"id": "ash-plain", "kind": "place", "name": "The ash plain", "deva": "", "ch": 4, "text": "The plain of Samyukt’s dream, grey ash under a black sky split by a fracture, with a weight too large for a body pressing behind it. Out of the ash rises a stringless black bow veined with violet light."}, {"id": "meditating-figure", "kind": "person", "name": "The meditating figure", "deva": "", "ch": 4, "text": "A figure meditating on the ash plain, with matted hair, a crescent moon and a trident, who asks, “What are you willing to destroy in order to protect what you love?” Samyukt’s answer, he says, “was not the question”."}, {"id": "damaru", "kind": "sign", "name": "The damaru", "deva": "डमरू", "ch": 4, "text": "A hand drum whose rhythm runs inside the slowed pulse of Samyukt’s dream. Waking in a draw posture, he plays the newest pulse’s audio and hears a damaru’s rhythm beneath the vibration, almost too deep to separate from his heartbeat."}, {"id": "dr-leela-menon", "kind": "person", "name": "Dr Leela Menon", "deva": "", "ch": 5, "text": "A seismologist with the international analysis group, whose data shows Kairavi’s acceleration too; she brings Kairavi into an emergency external-review group. Kairavi’s timeline is wrong, she says: the latest pulse moved even their most cautious range six days forward."}, {"id": "lines", "kind": "sign", "name": "The lines", "deva": "", "ch": 5, "text": "During the office pulse Kairavi sees faint lines along glass frames, ceiling joints and conduits, some taut, others broken, and a distortion around the dizziest people. To her they are not cracks but relationships; they vanish with the pulse."}, {"id": "western-corridor", "kind": "place", "name": "The western corridor", "deva": "", "ch": 5, "text": "When smoke cuts off the eastern stairwell, Kairavi leads people west, judging the pressure has passed. A light frame tears loose and strikes Varun as he pushes a colleague clear; he needs four stitches."}, {"id": "seven-concentrations", "kind": "sign", "name": "The seven concentrations", "deva": "", "ch": 5, "text": "Pressure signatures whose timing correlates worldwide but which organise locally, in seven vast regions, roughly one per continent, Antarctica least certain. The Asian region covers much of northern India, and the newest signals are gathering there."}, {"id": "preparations", "kind": "thing", "name": "The preparations", "deva": "", "ch": 5, "text": "Rajeev stocks rice and orders water, batteries and first-aid kits, as Kairavi advised, then asks her what else. She lists document copies, cash in small notes, power banks, shoes and medicines together, and a meeting place if phones fail."}], "cards": [{"id": "samyukt", "name": "Samyukt", "ch": 1, "line": "Runs his late father’s chemicals shop, Samar Chemicals, in a Dwarka market.", "facts": [{"ch": 1, "text": "Twenty-five; lives with his mother Naina and sister Ishita in a rented two-bedroom flat near the shop."}, {"ch": 1, "text": "Owes Mr Sethi on a five lakh rupee loan his father signed; he agrees to pay thirty-five thousand by Saturday."}, {"ch": 1, "text": "Tells Naina the payment is coming and the landlord is handled; he does not tell her about Mr Sethi."}, {"ch": 1, "text": "The night he first hears of the signals, a dull heat gathers beneath his ribs, then vanishes."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "Finally tells Raghav and Kabir he owes about twelve lakh: four to Raghav, three to Kabir, five tied to Sethi."}, {"ch": 4, "text": "After twelve days of fever and normal tests, he goes to the shop instead of resting; Naina takes his motorcycle keys."}, {"ch": 4, "text": "Agrees that nothing outside his friends’ repayment plan gets paid unless all three of them discuss it."}, {"ch": 4, "text": "Took up archery at twelve, inspired by Karna; Ishita says he sometimes confuses enduring everything with doing the right thing."}, {"ch": 4, "text": "At the range, heat runs from beneath his ribs into his arms and one arrow buries itself to the fletching."}, {"ch": 4, "text": "Wakes from a dream of an ash plain in a draw posture; the disturbance now feels like something calling him."}]}, {"id": "kairavi", "name": "Kairavi", "ch": 2, "line": "Works on machine-learning models for an international company in Dwarka.", "facts": [{"ch": 2, "text": "Finds a filter erasing Romanised Hindi from customer messages: real accuracy is eighty-seven point one per cent, not ninety-four point eight."}, {"ch": 2, "text": "Lives on the fourth floor of an apartment block with her parents, Rajeev and Meera, and her sister Aarohi."}, {"ch": 2, "text": "Pays into the household account; she transfers ten thousand extra and asks Rajeev not to commit her salary without asking."}, {"ch": 2, "text": "At twenty she had a place at a university abroad; her parents decided she should stay, and she still wants that future."}, {"ch": 2, "text": "Aligns four stations overnight and finds the gaps between pulses shrinking: the signal is accelerating."}, {"ch": 2, "text": "For a few seconds the graph’s lines look brighter than the screen; her fever reaches one hundred point four."}, {"ch": 5, "text": "Emails her models to seven institutions; Dr Leela Menon invites her into an emergency external-review group."}, {"ch": 5, "text": "During an office pulse she sees faint lines along the glass and ceiling, and gets most of the floor out."}, {"ch": 5, "text": "She leads a group into the western corridor, where a light frame strikes Varun; she tells him she was wrong."}, {"ch": 5, "text": "Lets Aarohi collect data under conditions: only sources Kairavi sends, no unknown attachments, stop at any fever."}]}, {"id": "raghav", "name": "Raghav", "ch": 1, "line": "Samyukt’s gaming friend, quick with a joke, who rushes into every fight.", "facts": [{"ch": 1, "text": "When Samyukt says the payment comes Friday, he jokes that tonight they will lose “because of skill, not depression”."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "Loses the flat he planned to buy after lending Samyukt four lakh rupees; he does not regret the loan."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "Plans to own a Porsche 911 Carrera and to start his own company, whose name he has told no one."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "After his mother left, it was just him and his father for years; he promises his father dinner tomorrow."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "At work, a process map is a chessboard to him; he reworks a software migration plan before six senior managers."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "Is sleeping badly and woke with aching hands; he blames the laptop, the drive, the flat, then age."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "Tells Samyukt: “I want to know when my friend is drowning before he asks me to lend him a boat.”"}, {"ch": 3, "text": "During the pulse he hears market voices with impossible clarity, each placed like a piece on a chessboard."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "By the car he hears Kabir say his name, though Kabir’s mouth has not moved."}, {"ch": 4, "text": "Builds a repayment spreadsheet with three cash-flow plans and insists nothing is paid outside it unless all three discuss it."}]}, {"id": "kabir", "name": "Kabir", "ch": 1, "line": "Samyukt’s quieter gaming friend, who remembers what people hope he has forgotten.", "facts": [{"ch": 1, "text": "Notices Samyukt sounds tired and asks whether the payment has come."}, {"ch": 1, "text": "Hears the seismic broadcast through Samyukt’s microphone; later he texts that it has happened again, same pattern, same interval."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "In his office’s electronics lab he builds a low-cost vibration-monitoring module for small factories."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "With the motor off and the rig isolated, his module records the seismic pattern three times."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "Has not shown the module to his manager, saying it was not ready, and resists presenting the pulses at a funding review."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "Has had a headache for three days; Rachna has a fever and two people in his office went home with body aches."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "Samyukt owes him three lakh rupees; at dinner he asks Samyukt to stop treating secrecy like protection."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "After the pulse, the module in his backpack glows faintly with an empty battery compartment."}, {"ch": 3, "text": "Denies calling Raghav’s name, then, as the pressure returns, says “Something is wrong.”"}, {"ch": 4, "text": "Scans the original loan papers; two handwritten additions lack Samar’s signature, and he urges Samyukt to get a legal opinion."}]}], "places": [{"id": "samar-chemicals", "name": "Samar Chemicals", "ch": 1, "text": "The industrial and housekeeping chemicals shop Samyukt’s late father founded; Samyukt haggles with a supplier, chases a stuck invoice and faces Mr Sethi here.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 418, "y": 332, "side": "end"}, {"id": "the-market", "name": "The market", "ch": 1, "text": "The bright, crowded market around the shop: pharmacies, coaching centres, food stalls and mobile shops, with scooters squeezing through gaps that do not exist.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 392, "y": 292, "side": "end"}, {"id": "samyukt-home", "name": "Samyukt’s home", "ch": 1, "text": "The rented two-bedroom flat where Samyukt lives with Naina and Ishita; from its balcony he feels the night air turn heavier.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 292, "y": 238, "side": "end"}, {"id": "the-hotel", "name": "The hotel", "ch": 1, "text": "Samyukt’s customer, whose payment for invoice 2147 is on hold until a revised invoice goes up on its portal.", "in_dwarka": false}, {"id": "the-hospital", "name": "The hospital", "ch": 1, "text": "Where Samyukt once drove his breathless father through traffic lights, a desperate journey that turned every earlier moment into a final one.", "in_dwarka": false}, {"id": "kairavi-office", "name": "Kairavi’s office", "ch": 2, "text": "The international company’s office where Kairavi finds a filter erasing Romanised Hindi and stops a flawed model being called ‘production-ready’.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 660, "y": 132, "side": "start"}, {"id": "chilli-potato-stall", "name": "Stall under the metro", "ch": 2, "text": "A crowded stall beneath the metro line where Kairavi and Mitali toast reduced accuracy, until Rajeev calls asking for fifteen thousand rupees.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 606, "y": 182, "side": "end"}, {"id": "metro-line", "name": "The metro line", "ch": 2, "text": "The Delhi Metro’s raised tracks on concrete pillars, plastered with old posters and humming with trains arriving overhead.", "in_dwarka": true, "kind": "line"}, {"id": "kairavi-home", "name": "Kairavi’s home", "ch": 2, "text": "The fourth-floor flat where Kairavi lives with Rajeev, Meera and Aarohi; over dinner she asks that Aarohi be allowed to choose her own course.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 238, "y": 474, "side": "end"}, {"id": "rajeev-supermarket", "name": "Rajeev’s supermarket", "ch": 2, "text": "Rajeev’s supermarket, where Kairavi once put back a pair of headphones that he later brought home for her.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 690, "y": 540, "side": "start"}, {"id": "university-abroad", "name": "The university abroad", "ch": 2, "text": "The university that accepted Kairavi, with a partial scholarship, before her family decided she should stay.", "in_dwarka": false}, {"id": "station-india", "name": "Indian seismic station", "ch": 2, "text": "One of four stations whose readings Kairavi aligns overnight; its recording of the pulse shows a longer tail.", "in_dwarka": false}, {"id": "station-chile", "name": "Chilean seismic station", "ch": 2, "text": "One of the four stations Kairavi aligns overnight; its reading of the pulse carries a second low-frequency band.", "in_dwarka": false}, {"id": "station-kenya", "name": "Kenyan seismic station", "ch": 2, "text": "Registered the pulse four seconds before Australia, as though it lay closer to a source that does not exist.", "in_dwarka": false}, {"id": "station-australia", "name": "Australian seismic station", "ch": 2, "text": "One of the four stations Kairavi aligns overnight; it registered the pulse four seconds after Kenya.", "in_dwarka": false}, {"id": "stations-indonesia", "name": "Indonesian seismic stations", "ch": 2, "text": "Among the stations confirming the signal: real, unlike any recorded earthquake, and with no common epicentre.", "in_dwarka": false}, {"id": "raghav-lost-flat", "name": "The flat Raghav lost", "ch": 3, "text": "The ninth-floor flat with good sunlight and terrible tiles that Raghav loses to a family with faster banking reflexes.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 726, "y": 334, "side": "start"}, {"id": "raghav-conference-room", "name": "Raghav’s meeting", "ch": 3, "text": "The conference room where Raghav shows six senior managers why their software migration plan will fail, and how to save it.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 672, "y": 236, "side": "start"}, {"id": "kabir-lab", "name": "Kabir’s lab", "ch": 3, "text": "Kabir’s office electronics lab, where his low-cost vibration module picks up the seismic pattern on an isolated test rig, three runs out of three.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 640, "y": 300, "side": "start"}, {"id": "roll-stall", "name": "The roll stall", "ch": 3, "text": "The stall across the market that sends the rolls Raghav and Kabir eat with Samyukt at his desk, before the talk about money.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 452, "y": 360, "side": "start"}, {"id": "market-parking", "name": "The parking area", "ch": 3, "text": "Where Kabir’s battery-less module glows, and Raghav hears his own name in Kabir’s voice though Kabir’s mouth never moved.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 400, "y": 372, "side": "end"}, {"id": "the-clinic", "name": "The clinic", "ch": 4, "text": "Where, after twelve days of fever, every test comes back normal and a doctor orders Samyukt to rest for two days.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 200, "y": 548, "side": "start"}, {"id": "archery-range", "name": "The archery range", "ch": 4, "text": "The range where Samyukt trained for years; rusty after three years away, he buries one arrow in the target up to its fletching.", "in_dwarka": true, "x": 372, "y": 566, "side": "start"}, {"id": "ash-plain", "name": "The plain of ash", "ch": 4, "text": "The plain of ash under a fractured sky in Samyukt’s dream, where a black bow veined with violet rises from the ash.", "in_dwarka": false}, {"id": "menon-analysis-group", "name": "Dr Menon’s analysis group", "ch": 5, "text": "The international analysis group Dr Leela Menon works with; it takes Kairavi into its emergency external-review group and tests her model.", "in_dwarka": false}, {"id": "seven-concentrations", "name": "The seven concentrations", "ch": 5, "text": "Seven vast pressure concentrations on Dr Menon’s map, roughly one per continent; Antarctica’s has the weakest coverage and the worst confidence.", "in_dwarka": false}, {"id": "northern-india-concentration", "name": "Over northern India", "ch": 5, "text": "The Asian concentration on Dr Menon’s map, covering much of northern India, where the newest signals are not passing through but gathering.", "in_dwarka": false}], "newsletter": {"action": "https://app.kit.com/forms/10046604/subscriptions", "field": "email_address"}, "instagram": "https://www.instagram.com/divyarann/", "disclaimer": [{"h": false, "t": "Divyarann is a work of fiction. Its names, characters, organisations, events and incidents are products of the author&#x27;s imagination or are used fictitiously. Any resemblance to actual persons, living or dead, or to actual events is entirely coincidental."}, {"h": false, "t": "Real cities, neighbourhoods and landmarks appear only as settings. What happens in them in this story is invented."}, {"h": false, "t": "The story draws on Indian mythology, the epics and folklore, and reimagines them freely as fantasy. It is not a retelling of, or a commentary on, any scripture or religious tradition, and it is not intended to offend the beliefs or sentiments of any person or community."}, {"h": false, "t": "The views and actions of the characters are their own and do not represent the views of the author."}, {"h": false, "t": "This is a draft web edition of Book One. Chapters may be revised, and this edition may be withdrawn when the book is published."}, {"h": false, "t": "© {year} Dhruv. All rights reserved. Please share a link to the site rather than copying or reposting chapters elsewhere."}], "authorBio": [{"h": true, "t": "A note from the author"}, {"h": false, "t": "Namaste to all my fellow readers. This book is my dream project: a painting that never had a canvas to be painted on. My aim with this story is to tell people about our culture and history in my own fictional way. I want to share my own learnings, discoveries and interests in Sanatan Dharma, and how deeply it has influenced my life. The book uses many terms from our sacred Vedas, our scriptures and our beloved stories."}, {"h": false, "t": "I would also like to sincerely thank everyone who supports me. No human being can achieve anything alone in this world. As the saying goes, &quot;Behind every successful man there is a woman,&quot; and that is true in my case as well. I have three beautiful and amazing women in my life, to whom I owe everything."}, {"h": false, "t": "My mother, who brought me into this beautiful world and has looked after me ever since."}, {"h": false, "t": "My sister, who supports me in everything I do and is always there for me."}, {"h": false, "t": "And finally, my girlfriend, who pulled me out of a very dark place and has loved and supported me since the day we met. I owe her the most!"}, {"h": false, "t": "Once again, I thank all my readers from the bottom of my heart."}, {"v": true, "h": false, "t": "ॐ भूर्भुवः स्वः<br>तत्स॑वि॒तुर्वरे॑ण्यं॒<br>भर्गो॑ दे॒वस्य॑ धीमहि।<br>धियो॒ यो नः॑ प्रचो॒दया॑त्॥"}], "year": 2026};
